\id MRK - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Mark
\toc1 The Good News According to Mark
\toc2 Mark
\toc3 Mrk
\mt1 Mark

\imt Introduction
\ip The writer never names himself. What the book itself shows is a man writing Greek for people who do not live inside Jewish practice. He stops the story to explain that eating with "common hands" means unwashed, and to describe the washings the tradition of the elders required, because his readers would not otherwise know (7:3-4). Every Aramaic phrase he keeps, he translates. He reckons the night by Roman watches, explains the value of a Jewish coin by giving its Roman equivalent, and uses Latin words carried over into Greek — legion, centurion, praetorium, quadrans. The name Mark comes from the title the early copies carry, not from anything the writer says about himself.
\ip Those same features, with the Latin thickest of all, point west — to Rome or somewhere near it. So does a small detail he lets slip: the man pressed into carrying the cross is identified as "the father of Alexander and Rufus" (15:21), which is only useful if the readers know Alexander and Rufus. As for when, the book is preoccupied with the destruction of the temple, which the Romans carried out in the year 70, and with what the followers of Jesus are to do when it comes. That puts the writing somewhere close to that war, in the sixties.
\ip These readers were under pressure. A long section of the book (chapter 13) is given to being handed over to councils, flogged in synagogues, made to stand before governors and kings, betrayed by relatives, hated by everyone — and to holding out to the end. The demand to take up a cross and follow is not a figure of speech in a city where crosses stood by the roads. They belonged to a movement whose founder Rome had executed as a rebel king, and they were being asked to explain how a crucified man could be God's anointed.
\ip This book is the answer, and it is told at a run. There is no birth story and no genealogy; it opens at full speed with a man shouting in the desert, and the word "immediately" drives it forward some forty times. Jesus silences everyone who names him correctly, and the men closest to him understand almost nothing until the end. The first human being in the whole account to say plainly who he is turns out to be the Roman officer in charge of killing him, and he says it at the cross. In the oldest copies the book stops at 16:8, with women running from an open tomb too frightened to tell anyone. The longer ending that later copies add is printed here and footnoted where it begins.

\c 1
\s1 The Beginning of the Good News
\p
\v 1 The beginning of the good news\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl euangelion\tl*. Two streams meet in the word. In the Greek Old Testament it is what a runner brings back from the battlefield — the war is won, the king is coming home: "How beautiful on the mountains are the feet of the one bringing good news, announcing peace... saying to Zion, Your God reigns" (Isaiah 52:7). In the Roman world it was the word for an imperial bulletin; an inscription of 9 BC calls the birthday of Augustus the beginning of \tl euangelia\tl* for the world. To open a book this way is to announce a reign, and to announce it over against another one.\f* of Jesus the Christ\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl Christos\tl*, translating Hebrew \tl mashiach\tl*, anointed one. Anointing with oil was how a man was installed in office — kings (1 Samuel 16:13), priests (Exodus 29:7). The word is a job title, not a surname: the one God has installed to rule.\f*, Son of God\f + \fr 1:1 \ft In Israel's scriptures the title belongs to Israel itself ("Israel is my firstborn son," Exodus 4:22) and to the king on David's throne (2 Samuel 7:14; Psalm 2:7). It names the one who stands for God and acts with his authority. This book puts it in the first line, God speaks it at the river (1:11) and on the mountain (9:7), and the last human voice in the story speaks it at the cross (15:39).\f*.
\s1 John in the Wilderness
\p
\v 2 As it is written in Isaiah the prophet\f + \fr 1:2 \ft What follows is stitched from more than one place: "I am sending my messenger before your face" is Malachi 3:1 with wording from Exodus 23:20, and the voice in the wilderness is Isaiah 40:3. Naming a composite quotation under the better-known prophet was ordinary practice. The choice of Isaiah is not idle — Isaiah 40 opens the great promise that the exile is over and the LORD is coming back to his people. That is the frame the whole book is set in.\f*: "Look, I am sending my messenger before your face, who will build your road\f + \fr 1:2 \ft In Malachi, God says the messenger goes before his own face. Here it is "before your face," addressed to Jesus. The one whose road is being built was the LORD himself.\f*;
\v 3 a voice of one shouting in the wilderness, 'Get the way of the Lord ready, make his paths straight'" —
\v 4 John came, immersing\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Greek \tl baptizō\tl*, to dip or plunge under. Washing by immersion was ordinary in Jewish practice — for ritual uncleanness, and for a Gentile coming into Israel. What was not ordinary was doing it to Israelites. Calling the covenant people out to the river and putting them under treats them as though they were standing outside and had to come back in.\f* in the wilderness and proclaiming an immersion of turning back\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Greek \tl metanoia\tl*, rendered here by the Hebrew idea behind it, \tl shub\tl* — to turn around and go back the way you came. It is what the prophets demanded of Israel (Hosea 14:1; Joel 2:12-13). Not a feeling of regret; a change of direction.\f* for the forgiveness of sins.
\v 5 And the whole Judean countryside and all the people of Jerusalem were going out to him, and they were being immersed by him in the Jordan river \add [i.e. the very water Israel crossed to enter the land]\add*, confessing their sins out loud.
\v 6 And John was clothed in camel's hair with a leather belt around his loins\f + \fr 1:6 \ft This is a costume with a name on it. "A hairy man, with a leather belt around his loins" is how Elijah is identified in 2 Kings 1:8, and Malachi had promised that Elijah would be sent before the great day of the LORD (Malachi 4:5). Nobody has to explain who this is. A reader sees him and knows.\f*, and he ate locusts and wild honey \add [i.e. what the desert gives, and nothing a town could supply]\add*.
\v 7 And he was proclaiming: "The one stronger than I is coming after me, and I am not fit to bend down and untie the strap of his sandals \add [i.e. the one task so low that even a slave could be spared it]\add*.
\v 8 I immersed you in water, but he will immerse you in the Holy Spirit."\f + \fr 1:8 \ft The prophets marked out the age of restoration by an outpouring of God's Spirit on his people (Joel 2:28-29; Ezekiel 36:26-27; Isaiah 44:3). John is saying his own work is only the preparation for that.\f*
\s1 The Immersion and the Testing
\p
\v 9 And it happened in those days that Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee \add [i.e. a village so small that no writing before this one so much as mentions it]\add* and was immersed into the Jordan by John.
\v 10 And immediately, coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens being torn open\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \tl schizō\tl*, to rip. Not a door opening — a tearing. Isaiah had cried out, "Oh that you would tear the heavens open and come down!" (Isaiah 64:1). The word occurs only twice in this book: here, and at the moment Jesus dies, when the curtain of the sanctuary is torn from top to bottom (15:38). Both times a barrier between heaven and earth is ripped, and both times a voice declares that he is God's Son.\f* and the Spirit coming down into him like a dove \add [i.e. this is the anointing that makes him in fact what the first line of this book calls him]\add*.
\v 11 And a voice came out of the heavens: "You are my Son, the beloved. In you I have taken delight."\f + \fr 1:11 \ft Two scriptures welded into one sentence. "You are my Son" is Psalm 2:7, spoken over the king of David's line at his crowning. "In you I have taken delight" is Isaiah 42:1, the opening of the servant songs — and that servant's road runs through suffering (Isaiah 52:13-53:12). The voice names him king and servant at once, and the rest of the book is about how those turn out to be the same office.\f*
\p
\v 12 And immediately the Spirit drove him out\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Greek \tl ekballō\tl*, the verb used everywhere else in this book for throwing a demon out of a man. He is not led; he is propelled.\f* into the wilderness.
\v 13 And he was in the wilderness forty days \add [i.e. as Israel was forty years in the wilderness, and Moses forty days on the mountain]\add*, being tested by Satan, and he was with the wild animals\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Not "among" but "with" — the preposition of company. In the prophets the wild beasts are the danger of the desert (Ezekiel 34:25), and they are also the sign of the world put right, when wolf and lamb lie down together (Isaiah 11:6-9). He is in the place of danger, and he is at peace in it.\f*, and the angels were serving him.
\s1 The Kingdom of God Has Come Near
\p
\v 14 And after John was handed over\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Greek \tl paradidōmi\tl*, to hand over, deliver up. It is the word this book uses again and again for what is done to Jesus — by Judas, by the priests, by Pilate (9:31; 10:33; 14:10, 41; 15:1, 15) — and for what will be done to his followers (13:9-12). The forerunner's arrest is the first link in that chain.\f*, Jesus came into Galilee proclaiming the good news of God
\v 15 and saying, "The time has been filled full \add [i.e. the appointed moment has arrived — not drawn nearer, arrived]\add* and the kingdom of God has come near\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Not a territory and not a place after death. The word means God's kingship, God reigning. Israel's hope was that the LORD would take up his rule in the open and set the world right (Psalm 145:11-13; Daniel 2:44; Isaiah 52:7). To say it has come near is to say the waiting is over.\f*. Turn back, and trust in the good news."
\p
\v 16 And passing along beside the sea of Galilee, he saw Simon and Andrew the brother of Simon casting a net in the sea, for they were fishermen.
\v 17 And Jesus said to them, "Come after me, and I will make you become fishers of men."\f + \fr 1:17 \ft The image is out of the prophets, and there it has an edge on it: God sends for "many fishers" and they catch his people and haul them in (Jeremiah 16:16; compare Amos 4:2; Habakkuk 1:14-17). It is the language of a great gathering-up, and it is being handed to four men who catch fish for a living.\f*
\v 18 And immediately they left the nets and followed him.
\v 19 And going on a little farther, he saw James the son of Zebedee and John his brother — they too were in the boat, putting the nets in order —
\v 20 and immediately he called them. And they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men \add [i.e. the family and the family business in one stroke]\add* and went off after him.
\s1 A Sabbath in Capernaum
\p
\v 21 And they went into Capernaum. And immediately on the Sabbath he went into the synagogue and was teaching.
\v 22 And they were struck out of themselves\f + \fr 1:22 \ft Greek \tl ekplēssō\tl*, to be knocked clean out of one's senses. It is a violent word, and this book keeps it for the effect Jesus has on a crowd.\f* at his teaching, for he was teaching them as one who has authority, and not as the scribes \add [i.e. a scribe taught by citing which teacher had said what before him; this man cited no one]\add*.
\v 23 And immediately there was in their synagogue a man in an unclean spirit, and he screamed out,
\v 24 saying, "What is there between us and you \add [i.e. what business have you with us]\add*, Jesus the Nazarene? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are — the holy one of God."\f + \fr 1:24 \ft "What is there between us and you" is a stock protest against unwanted interference (Judges 11:12; 1 Kings 17:18; 2 Kings 3:13). Watch who is speaking. All through the first half of this book the unclean spirits know exactly who Jesus is, and no human being does.\f*
\v 25 And Jesus rebuked him, saying, "Be muzzled\f + \fr 1:25 \ft Greek \tl phimoō\tl*, the word for putting a muzzle on an ox. "Rebuked," just before it, is the verb the Greek Old Testament uses for God's shout at hostile powers — at the sea (Psalm 104:7; Nahum 1:4) and at the accuser himself (Zechariah 3:2). He will use both words again, on the sea, at 4:39.\f*, and come out of him."
\v 26 And the unclean spirit threw him into convulsions and gave a great shout and came out of him.
\v 27 And they were all so shaken that they argued it out among themselves, saying, "What is this? A new teaching, with authority! He gives orders even to the unclean spirits, and they obey him."
\v 28 And the report of him went out immediately, everywhere, into the whole surrounding country of Galilee.
\p
\v 29 And immediately they came out of the synagogue and went into the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John.
\v 30 Now Simon's mother-in-law was lying down with a fever, and immediately they spoke to him about her.
\v 31 And he came to her and raised her up, taking hold of her hand; and the fever left her, and she served them \add [i.e. she was on her feet and waiting on the table, which is how completely it was gone]\add*.
\p
\v 32 And when evening came, when the sun had set \add [i.e. the Sabbath was over, and they could carry a sick man again without breaking it]\add*, they carried to him all who were badly off and those who were demon-possessed.
\v 33 And the whole city was gathered together at the door.
\v 34 And he healed many who were badly off with all kinds of diseases, and he threw out many demons, and he would not let the demons speak, because they knew him \add [i.e. he refuses their testimony, accurate as it is]\add*.
\s1 He Moves On Through Galilee
\p
\v 35 And very early, while it was still night, he got up and went out and went off to a deserted place, and there he was praying.
\v 36 And Simon and those with him hunted him down,
\v 37 and they found him and said to him, "Everyone is looking for you."
\v 38 And he said to them, "Let us go elsewhere, into the neighboring towns, so that I may proclaim there also, for that is what I came out for \add [i.e. the proclaiming is the errand; the healings serve it]\add*."
\v 39 And he went, proclaiming in their synagogues through the whole of Galilee, and throwing out the demons.
\s1 He Touches a Leper
\p
\v 40 And a leper\f + \fr 1:40 \ft The word covers a range of skin diseases, and the trouble was never only medical. Leviticus 13-14 put such a man outside the camp with his clothes torn, calling out "Unclean, unclean" to warn off anyone who came near. He is cut off from his family, his village, and the worship of his God, and there is no route back except a priest's verdict that the disease has gone.\f* came to him, begging him and going down on his knees, saying to him, "If you are willing, you can cleanse me."
\v 41 And his heart went out to him\f + \fr 1:41 \ft Greek \tl splanchnizomai\tl*, from the word for the inward organs — the gut turns over. A number of manuscripts read instead "he was angry," and if that is what was written, the anger is at the disease and the exile it imposes, not at the man.\f*, and he stretched out his hand and touched him \add [i.e. the one thing nobody had done since the disease began — and by the law the touch should have made him unclean, not the man clean]\add* and said to him, "I am willing. Be cleansed."
\v 42 And immediately the leprosy left him, and he was cleansed.
\v 43 And he spoke to him harshly and immediately drove him out,
\v 44 and said to him, "See that you say nothing to anyone, but go, show yourself to the priest and bring for your cleansing what Moses laid down\f + \fr 1:44 \ft Leviticus 14:1-32 lays out the whole procedure by which a cleansed man is examined, offers his sacrifices, and is readmitted to the community. He is not being told to go around the law. He is being sent to finish it.\f*, as a witness to them \add [i.e. let them see for themselves what has happened]\add*."
\v 45 But he went out and began to proclaim it far and wide and to spread the word, so that Jesus could no longer go openly into a city, but was outside in deserted places; and they kept coming to him from every direction \add [i.e. the man is back inside the town and the one who put him there is out in the wilderness]\add*.

\c 2
\s1 The Son of Man Forgives Sins on the Earth
\p
\v 1 And when he went into Capernaum again after some days, it was heard that he was at home.
\v 2 And many gathered together, so that there was no more room, not even at the door, and he was speaking the word to them.
\v 3 And they came, bringing to him a paralyzed man, carried by four men.
\v 4 And when they could not bring him near because of the crowd, they took off the roof above where he was, and when they had dug through it they let down the mat on which the paralyzed man was lying \add [i.e. a flat roof of mud packed over beams, reached by the stair outside — they broke a hole in a man's house]\add*.
\v 5 And when Jesus saw their trust \add [i.e. saw it in what they had just done — it is not a feeling he read off their faces]\add*, he said to the paralyzed man, "Child, your sins are forgiven \add [i.e. they stand forgiven; he does not say that he is doing it, but that it is done]\add*."
\v 6 But some of the scribes were sitting there and reasoning in their hearts,
\v 7 "Why does this man talk like this? He blasphemes. Who can forgive sins but one — God?"\f + \fr 2:7 \ft They are not being obtuse. "I, I am the one who wipes out your wrongdoings for my own sake" (Isaiah 43:25) — forgiving is God's to do and nobody else's. So either this man is exercising an authority that belongs to God, or he is a blasphemer. Their logic allows no third possibility, and this book never offers one.\f*
\v 8 And immediately Jesus knew in his spirit that they were reasoning this way among themselves, and he said to them, "Why are you reasoning these things in your hearts?
\v 9 Which is easier — to say to the paralyzed man, 'Your sins are forgiven,' or to say, 'Get up and pick up your mat and walk'? \add [i.e. easier to say, because no one can check it]\add*
\v 10 But so that you may know that the son of man\f + \fr 2:10 \ft The phrase comes from Daniel 7:13-14, where "one like a son of man" comes with the clouds to the Ancient of Days and is given dominion, honor, and a kingdom that will not pass away. It is Jesus' own name for himself all through this book, and it lets him claim everything in Daniel's vision without using a title the crowd would hear as a summons to arms. What is startling here is the address: that authority is being exercised "on the earth," in a crowded house in Capernaum, now.\f* has authority on the earth to forgive sins" — he said to the paralyzed man,
\v 11 "I say to you, get up, pick up your mat, and go to your house."
\v 12 And he got up, and immediately he picked up the mat and went out in front of them all, so that they were all beside themselves and glorified God, saying, "We have never seen anything like this."
\s1 Levi, and a Table Full of Sinners
\p
\v 13 And he went out beside the sea again; and the whole crowd was coming to him, and he was teaching them.
\v 14 And as he was passing along he saw Levi the son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax booth\f + \fr 2:14 \ft A customs post on the trade road running through Capernaum, collecting tolls for Herod Antipas. The men who worked them were counted with the outsiders on three grounds at once: they took money for the ruling power, they took more than was owed, and they handled Gentile coin all day long. Calling one of them is not a gesture of tolerance. It is a scandal.\f*, and he said to him, "Follow me." And he got up and followed him.
\p
\v 15 And it happened that he was reclining at table in Levi's house \add [i.e. at a full meal, and in that world you ate with the people you were willing to be counted among]\add*, and many tax collectors and sinners were reclining with Jesus and his disciples, for there were many of them and they were following him.
\v 16 And the scribes of the Pharisees, seeing that he was eating with the sinners and tax collectors, said to his disciples, "He eats with the tax collectors and sinners?"
\v 17 And when Jesus heard it he said to them, "It is not the strong who need a healer, but those who are badly off. I did not come to call righteous men, but sinners \add [i.e. this table is not a lapse in my work; it is my work]\add*."
\s1 The Bridegroom Is Here
\p
\v 18 Now John's disciples and the Pharisees were fasting.\f + \fr 2:18 \ft Beyond the one fast the law required, the devout fasted twice a week; it was the mark of a serious man. Both the strictest party in Israel and the followers of the prophet in the wilderness kept it. The question that follows is not petty.\f* And they came and said to him, "Why do John's disciples and the disciples of the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast?"
\v 19 And Jesus said to them, "Can the sons of the wedding hall \add [i.e. the wedding party]\add* fast while the bridegroom is with them?\f + \fr 2:19 \ft A wedding ran seven days, and for that week the guests were released from ordinary religious obligations; fasting at a wedding would insult the host. But hear what he has just made himself. In the prophets the bridegroom of Israel is God: "your Maker is your husband" (Isaiah 54:5); "as a bridegroom rejoices over the bride, so your God will rejoice over you" (Isaiah 62:5); "I will betroth you to me forever" (Hosea 2:19).\f* As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast.
\v 20 But days will come when the bridegroom is taken away from them \add [i.e. taken by force — this is not a departure he chooses]\add*, and then they will fast, in that day.
\v 21 No one sews a patch of unshrunk cloth onto an old garment. If he does, the new filling pulls away from the old, and the tear becomes worse.
\v 22 And no one puts new wine into old wineskins. If he does, the wine will burst the skins, and the wine is lost and the skins as well. New wine goes into fresh skins."\f + \fr 2:22 \ft New wine is still working, and the gas it gives off will split a skin that has already been stretched once and gone hard. Neither picture says the old thing was bad. Both say it cannot hold what is now being poured.\f*
\s1 Lord Even of the Sabbath
\p
\v 23 And it happened that on the Sabbath he was going through the grainfields, and his disciples began to make their way along, pulling off the heads of grain.
\v 24 And the Pharisees said to him, "Look, why are they doing on the Sabbath what is not permitted?"\f + \fr 2:24 \ft Pulling heads of grain from a neighbor's field by hand was expressly allowed (Deuteronomy 23:25), so the charge is not theft. Under the Pharisees' reckoning, plucking grain counted as reaping; reaping was work; work was forbidden on the Sabbath.\f*
\v 25 And he said to them, "Have you never read what David did when he was in need and hungry, he and those with him —
\v 26 how he went into the house of God in the time of Abiathar the high priest and ate the bread of the Presence, which no one is permitted to eat except the priests, and gave some also to those who were with him?"\f + \fr 2:26 \ft 1 Samuel 21:1-6. David, already anointed king but hunted by the reigning one, came to the sanctuary hungry and was given the bread of the Presence — the twelve loaves set out weekly before the LORD, which by Leviticus 24:5-9 only the priests could eat. The priest who handed it over was Ahimelech; Abiathar was his son, and that whole episode is what put Abiathar at David's side for the rest of his reign, so naming him may be a way of pointing at the passage rather than the moment. Note what the precedent actually is: God's anointed, not yet on the throne, sets aside a regulation, and those with him eat because he does.\f*
\v 27 And he said to them, "The Sabbath came into being for man, and not man for the Sabbath.
\v 28 So the son of man is lord even of the Sabbath \add [i.e. lord over an institution God himself established]\add*."

\c 3
\s1 Doing Good on the Sabbath, and the Plot to Kill Him
\p
\v 1 And he went into the synagogue again. And there was a man there with a withered hand.
\v 2 And they were watching him closely, to see whether he would heal him on the Sabbath, so that they might bring a charge against him \add [i.e. they were not there to learn; they were gathering evidence]\add*.
\v 3 And he said to the man who had the withered hand, "Get up, into the middle \add [i.e. out where the whole assembly must look at him]\add*."
\v 4 And he said to them, "Is it permitted on the Sabbath to do good or to do harm, to rescue a life or to kill?" \add [i.e. there is no third option — if good can be done and you refuse, the refusing is the harm]\add* But they kept quiet.
\v 5 And he looked around at them with anger, grieved through and through at the hardness of their heart\f + \fr 3:5 \ft Greek \tl pōrōsis\tl*, a callus — flesh gone hard and past feeling. It is the condition of Pharaoh, who watched God act again and again and would not yield (Exodus 7-9), and of the house of Israel that would not listen (Ezekiel 3:7). It is not ignorance; it is having seen and refusing what you saw. The same word turns up twice more in this book, and both times it is said of the disciples (6:52; 8:17).\f*, and he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and his hand was put back as it was.
\v 6 And the Pharisees went out and immediately took counsel with the Herodians against him, how they might destroy him.\f + \fr 3:6 \ft An unnatural alliance. The Pharisees were the party of strict Torah; the Herodians were the men around Herod Antipas, Rome's client ruler in Galilee. They had nothing in common but the man in front of them, who threatened both. And note the day: he has just asked whether the Sabbath is for saving life or killing, and they walk out and spend it planning a killing.\f*
\s1 Crowds from Every Direction
\p
\v 7 And Jesus withdrew with his disciples to the sea, and a great crowd from Galilee followed; and from Judea
\v 8 and from Jerusalem and from Idumea and beyond the Jordan and around Tyre and Sidon \add [i.e. from every direction at once, Jewish country and Gentile alike]\add*, a great crowd came to him when they heard all that he was doing.
\v 9 And he told his disciples to keep a small boat ready for him because of the crowd, so that they would not press in on him;
\v 10 for he healed many, so that everyone with a scourge upon him \add [i.e. everyone with a disease flogging him]\add* was falling on him to touch him.
\v 11 And the unclean spirits, whenever they saw him, fell down before him and screamed out, saying, "You are the Son of God."
\v 12 And he rebuked them hard, that they should not make him known \add [i.e. what they say is true, and he will not have it said by them]\add*.
\s1 He Appoints Twelve
\p
\v 13 And he went up the mountain and called to himself those he himself wanted, and they came to him.
\v 14 And he made twelve\f + \fr 3:14 \ft The number is the argument. Israel was twelve tribes; to appoint twelve is to lay the foundation of a people, not to hire a staff. The mountain says the same thing — it is where Moses went up and where Israel was made a nation (Exodus 24:4, where Moses builds an altar with twelve pillars for the twelve tribes).\f*, whom he also named sent ones\f + \fr 3:14 \ft Greek \tl apostolos\tl*, one dispatched carrying the sender's authority — the word behind "apostle." It says what they are for, not what rank they hold.\f*, so that they might be with him, and so that he might send them out to proclaim
\v 15 and to have authority to throw out the demons.
\v 16 And he made the twelve, and he laid on Simon the name Peter\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Greek \tl Petros\tl*, Rock. Giving a man a new name is an act of authority over him, as when God renamed Abram and Jacob (Genesis 17:5; 32:28).\f*;
\v 17 and James the son of Zebedee and John the brother of James, and he laid on them the name Boanerges, that is, Sons of Thunder;
\v 18 and Andrew and Philip and Bartholomew and Matthew and Thomas and James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus and Simon the Cananaean\f + \fr 3:18 \ft Not a man from Cana: the word is Aramaic for "the zealous one." If it marks a political allegiance, then the twelve holds a man sworn to armed resistance against Rome standing beside Matthew, who collected Rome's money.\f*,
\v 19 and Judas Iscariot, who also handed him over \add [i.e. the reader is told how this one ends before his story has begun]\add*.
\s1 They Say He Is Out of His Mind
\p
\v 20 And he came into a house, and the crowd came together again, so that they could not even eat bread.
\v 21 And when his own people \add [i.e. his family]\add* heard of it, they went out to seize him \add [i.e. to take him into custody by force]\add*, for they were saying, "He is out of his mind."
\s1 By Whose Power Does He Throw Out Demons?
\p
\v 22 And the scribes who had come down from Jerusalem were saying, "He has Beelzebul," and, "By the ruler of the demons he throws out the demons."\f + \fr 3:22 \ft The name goes back to Baal-zebub, god of Ekron, whom a king of Israel once sent to consult instead of the LORD and was condemned for it (2 Kings 1:2-3). By this period it stood for the chief of the demons. Note also who brings the charge: a delegation come down from the capital, not a local complaint. This is the official verdict, and the verdict is that his power is the enemy's power.\f*
\v 23 And he called them to himself and said to them in parables, "How can Satan throw out Satan?
\v 24 And if a kingdom is divided against itself, that kingdom cannot stand.
\v 25 And if a house is divided against itself, that house will not be able to stand.
\v 26 And if Satan has risen up against himself and is divided, he cannot stand — he has an end \add [i.e. on your account he is already finished, which would be news worth hearing]\add*.
\v 27 But no one can go into the strong man's house and plunder his goods unless he first ties up the strong man; then he will plunder his house.\f + \fr 3:27 \ft Isaiah asked whether the prey can be taken from a mighty man, and God answered that he himself would take it and rescue the captives (Isaiah 49:24-25). That is the work being described. And the tying up is spoken of as already done: every exorcism these men have watched is the looting of a house whose owner is already bound.\f*
\v 28 Amen, I say to you, everything will be forgiven the sons of men, the sins and the blasphemies, however much they blaspheme;
\v 29 but whoever blasphemes against the Holy Spirit has no forgiveness into the age, but is guilty of an everlasting sin" —
\v 30 because they were saying, "He has an unclean spirit."\f + \fr 3:29 \ft The last line of the paragraph tells you what this is: they were saying he has an unclean spirit. To look straight at the work of God's Spirit — a man set free in front of you — and call it the enemy's work is not one sin among the rest. It destroys the very instrument by which a man could ever come to see that he was wrong, since the evidence that would convict him has been filed as proof of his case. Nothing is left to bring him back. The door is not shut from God's side.\f*
\s1 Who Are My Mother and My Brothers?
\p
\v 31 And his mother and his brothers came, and standing outside they sent to him, calling him.
\v 32 And a crowd was sitting around him, and they said to him, "Look, your mother and your brothers are outside, looking for you."
\v 33 And he answered them, "Who is my mother, and my brothers?"
\v 34 And he looked around at those sitting in a circle around him and said, "Look — my mother and my brothers.
\v 35 Whoever does the will of God, this one is my brother and sister and mother \add [i.e. the family is being redrawn, and the line runs between those sitting inside around him and those standing outside sending word in]\add*."

\c 4
\s1 The Sower, the Seed, and the Soils
\p
\v 1 And again he began to teach beside the sea. And a very great crowd gathered to him, so that he got into a boat and sat in it out on the sea, and the whole crowd was on the land facing the sea.
\v 2 And he taught them many things in parables\f + \fr 4:2 \ft Greek \tl parabolē\tl*, standing in for the Hebrew \tl mashal\tl* — a comparison, a riddle, a saying with a hook in it. A \tl mashal\tl* is not an illustration that makes a hard thing easy. It hands the hearer something he has to work at, and what he does with it shows what he is. Ezekiel's hearers dismissed him with exactly this word: "he is only telling parables" (Ezekiel 20:49).\f*, and in his teaching he said to them:
\v 3 "Listen. Look, the sower went out to sow.
\v 4 And it happened as he sowed that some fell beside the road, and the birds came and ate it up \add [i.e. seed was broadcast first and plowed in afterward, so the path and the stones and the thorns all get their share]\add*.
\v 5 And other fell on the rocky ground \add [i.e. a shelf of bedrock under a thin skin of soil]\add*, where it did not have much soil, and immediately it sprang up, because it had no depth of soil.
\v 6 And when the sun rose it was scorched, and because it had no root it withered.
\v 7 And other fell into the thorns, and the thorns came up and choked it, and it gave no fruit.
\v 8 And others fell into the good soil and gave fruit, coming up and growing, and bore thirty and sixty and a hundred."\f + \fr 4:8 \ft Sevenfold to tenfold was a good harvest in that country. Thirty is remarkable, and a hundred is what Isaac reaped in the year the LORD blessed him (Genesis 26:12). These are not modest numbers. They are what God's own doing looks like.\f*
\v 9 And he said, "Whoever has ears to hear, let him hear."
\s1 Why He Speaks in Parables
\p
\v 10 And when he was alone, those around him with the twelve asked him about the parables.
\v 11 And he said to them, "To you the secret\f + \fr 4:11 \ft Greek \tl mystērion\tl*, which in Daniel renders the Aramaic \tl raz\tl*: not an esoteric teaching for initiates but God's own plan, hidden until he chooses to disclose it (Daniel 2:28-29). The secret here is that God's reign has arrived — and that it has arrived like this, in a Galilean teacher who eats with tax collectors and is going to be killed.\f* of the kingdom of God has been given; but to those outside \add [i.e. those who have kept their distance — the scribes who called him Satan's man, the family standing out in the street]\add*, everything comes in parables,
\v 12 so that seeing they may see and not perceive, and hearing they may hear and not understand, or else they might turn back and it be forgiven them."\f + \fr 4:12 \ft Isaiah 6:9-10, the commission God gave Isaiah: go and preach to a people who will hear and not understand. It was not God blinding a willing people. Five chapters of Isaiah have already set out what these people had done; the preaching would harden what was already hard. The parables work the same way, and they work both directions at once — they open the thing to anyone who comes close and asks, and they close it to anyone who has already settled the question. And the hardening in Isaiah was not God's last word: the same chapter ends with a stump that is holy seed (Isaiah 6:13).\f*
\s1 He Explains the Parable
\p
\v 13 And he said to them, "Do you not know this parable? Then how will you know all the parables?
\v 14 The sower sows the word \add [i.e. the announcement that God's reign has arrived — the same "word" he has been speaking since 1:14]\add*.
\v 15 These are the ones beside the road, where the word is sown: when they hear, immediately Satan comes and takes away the word that was sown into them.
\v 16 And these are the ones sown on the rocky ground: when they hear the word, immediately they take it with joy \add [i.e. the welcome is real; nothing here is pretended]\add*,
\v 17 and they have no root in themselves but last only a season; then, when pressure or persecution comes because of the word, immediately they are tripped up.\f + \fr 4:17 \ft Greek \tl skandalizō\tl*, from the stick that springs a trap. Not a doubt and not a mistake — something set in the path that brings a man down. And notice what does it: nothing but pressure from outside, meeting no root inside.\f*
\v 18 And others are the ones sown into the thorns. These are the ones who have heard the word,
\v 19 and the cares of the age \add [i.e. the anxieties of getting through ordinary life under the present order]\add* and the deceit of riches and the cravings for everything else come in and choke the word, and it turns out fruitless \add [i.e. nothing dramatic happens; the plant is simply strangled by what grows up beside it]\add*.
\v 20 And those are the ones sown on the good soil, who hear the word and take it in and bear fruit — thirty and sixty and a hundred."
\s1 Nothing Is Hidden Except to Be Made Plain
\p
\v 21 And he said to them, "Does the lamp come in to be put under the measuring basket \add [i.e. a grain measure — nobody lights a lamp and puts a bucket over it]\add* or under the bed? Is it not to be put on the lampstand?
\v 22 For nothing is hidden except to be made plain, and nothing was covered up except to come out into the open \add [i.e. the hiding is for the sake of the showing, and it is temporary]\add*.
\v 23 If anyone has ears to hear, let him hear."
\v 24 And he said to them, "Watch what you hear. With the measure you measure out, it will be measured to you \add [i.e. the attention you bring is the attention you get back]\add*, and more will be added to you.
\v 25 For whoever has, to him it will be given; and whoever does not have, even what he has will be taken away from him \add [i.e. hearing runs one way or the other; there is no standing still in it]\add*."
\s1 Two More Seeds
\p
\v 26 And he said, "The kingdom of God is like this: as a man throws the seed on the ground,
\v 27 and sleeps and gets up, night and day, and the seed sprouts and grows tall — how, he himself does not know.
\v 28 Of itself the earth bears fruit: first the blade, then the head, then the full grain in the head.
\v 29 But when the fruit hands itself over, immediately he sends in the sickle, because the harvest has come."\f + \fr 4:29 \ft "Put in the sickle, for the harvest is ripe" (Joel 3:13). In the prophets the harvest is the day God comes to reckon. The farmer does nothing at all through the whole middle of this story, and then acts at once, because the time has come and not before.\f*
\p
\v 30 And he said, "How shall we liken the kingdom of God, or in what parable shall we set it?
\v 31 Like a grain of mustard, which when it is sown on the ground is smaller than all the seeds on the earth,
\v 32 and when it is sown, it comes up and becomes greater than all the garden plants and makes great branches, so that the birds of the sky can nest under its shade."\f + \fr 4:32 \ft The great tree with birds nesting in its branches is the standard picture of an empire that shelters the nations — Ezekiel's cedar planted on the high mountain (Ezekiel 17:22-24), Nebuchadnezzar's tree (Daniel 4:12). The substitution is deliberate: not a cedar, a mustard bush out of a kitchen garden. Everything the prophets promised, beginning from something nobody would look at twice.\f*
\p
\v 33 And with many such parables he was speaking the word to them, as they were able to hear it.
\v 34 And he did not speak to them without a parable, but privately he untied it all \add [i.e. unknotted every one of them]\add* for his own disciples.
\s1 The Wind and the Sea Obey Him
\p
\v 35 And on that day, when evening came, he said to them, "Let us cross over to the other side."
\v 36 And leaving the crowd, they took him along just as he was, in the boat; and other boats were with him.
\v 37 And a great windstorm came, and the waves were breaking into the boat, so that the boat was already filling up.
\v 38 And he himself was in the stern, asleep on the cushion.\f + \fr 4:38 \ft The detail is exact: in the stern, on the steersman's cushion. There is one other man in the scriptures asleep in a boat that is about to break up while everyone around him panics and prays (Jonah 1:4-6).\f* And they woke him and said to him, "Teacher, does it not matter to you that we are perishing?"
\v 39 And he woke up and rebuked the wind and said to the sea, "Quiet! Be muzzled!" And the wind dropped, and there was a great calm.\f + \fr 4:39 \ft The same two words he used on the unclean spirit in the synagogue (1:25). And the act itself belongs in Israel's scriptures to one person only: "You rule the raging of the sea; when its waves rise, you still them" (Psalm 89:9); "he stilled the storm, and the waves of the sea were hushed" (Psalm 107:29); "thus far you shall come, and no farther" (Job 38:11). No prophet does this and no king does this.\f*
\v 40 And he said to them, "Why are you cowards? Do you still have no trust?"
\v 41 And they feared a great fear and said to one another, "Who then is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?" \add [i.e. the question the whole book has been driving at, asked at last by the men closest to him]\add*

\c 5
\s1 Legion, and the Herd in the Sea
\p
\v 1 And they came to the other side of the sea, into the country of the Gerasenes \add [i.e. Gentile country, one of the Greek cities east of the Jordan]\add*.
\v 2 And when he came out of the boat, immediately a man in an unclean spirit met him out of the tombs.
\v 3 He had his dwelling among the tombs\f + \fr 5:3 \ft Under the law, contact with a corpse or a grave carries the gravest uncleanness there is; a man who touches one is unclean seven days (Numbers 19:16). This man does not touch a tomb, he lives in them. Isaiah's picture of paganism at its lowest is exactly this — people who sit among the graves and eat the flesh of pigs (Isaiah 65:4), and there is a herd of pigs on the hillside a few verses from here.\f*, and no one could bind him anymore, not even with a chain,
\v 4 because he had often been bound with fetters and chains, and he had torn the chains apart and smashed the fetters, and no one was strong enough to tame him \add [i.e. the word is the one used for breaking an animal; that is how they had come to handle him]\add*.
\v 5 And all the time, night and day, among the tombs and in the hills, he was screaming and cutting himself with stones.
\v 6 And when he saw Jesus from far off he ran and fell down before him
\v 7 and screamed with a great voice, "What is there between me and you, Jesus, Son of the Most High God\f + \fr 5:7 \ft "Most High" is the name by which outsiders acknowledge Israel's God in the scriptures — Melchizedek the Canaanite priest (Genesis 14:18-20), Nebuchadnezzar the Babylonian king (Daniel 3:26; 4:2). On Gentile soil, the spirit uses the Gentile title. Once again the unclean spirits know precisely who he is while the men in the boat are still asking (4:41).\f*? I put you under oath by God \add [i.e. the spirit swears him by the very God whose Son he has just called him]\add* — do not torture me!"
\v 8 For he had been saying to him, "Come out of the man, you unclean spirit."
\v 9 And he asked him, "What is your name?" And he said to him, "Legion is my name, for we are many."\f + \fr 5:9 \ft A Latin word carried into Greek, and not a vague one: a legion was the main formation of the Roman army, five to six thousand men. A first-century reader living under occupation would not have to be told what the word conjures. The man's own name is gone; he answers with the name of an army encamped in him.\f*
\v 10 And he begged him hard not to send them out of the country.
\v 11 Now there was a great herd of pigs feeding there on the hillside \add [i.e. no Jewish village would keep such a herd; this settles where they are standing]\add*.
\v 12 And they begged him, saying, "Send us into the pigs, so that we may go into them."
\v 13 And he let them. And the unclean spirits came out and went into the pigs, and the herd rushed down the steep bank into the sea — about two thousand of them — and they drowned in the sea.\f + \fr 5:13 \ft The scene is built out of Israel's oldest memory: a force with a military name, numbering in the thousands, that had held God's people captive, is driven into the sea and drowned there. "Pharaoh's chariots and his army he cast into the sea" (Exodus 15:4). It also shows what the spirits had been doing to the man all along — given any body at all, they drive it to destruction.\f*
\s1 They Beg Him to Leave
\p
\v 14 And the men feeding them ran and reported it in the city and in the countryside; and people came to see what had happened.
\v 15 And they came to Jesus and saw the demon-possessed man sitting, clothed and in his right mind — the one who had had the legion — and they were afraid.
\v 16 And those who had seen it told them what had happened to the demon-possessed man, and about the pigs.
\v 17 And they began to beg him to go away from their borders \add [i.e. they have seen a man given his life back, and what they want is for the power that did it to leave]\add*.
\v 18 And as he was getting into the boat, the man who had been demon-possessed begged him that he might be with him.
\v 19 And he did not let him, but said to him, "Go to your house, to your own people, and report to them all that the Lord has done for you, and how he had mercy on you."
\v 20 And he went off and began to proclaim in the Decapolis \add [i.e. the league of ten Greek cities east of the lake]\add* all that Jesus had done for him, and everyone marveled.\f + \fr 5:20 \ft Two things are worth holding together here. In Jewish country Jesus orders silence at every turn (1:44; 5:43; 7:36); here, on Gentile soil, he orders a man to go and tell. And he told him to report what the Lord had done — the word that stands for God's own name in the Greek scriptures — while what the man actually proclaims is what Jesus had done. The writer lets the substitution stand without a word of comment.\f*
\s1 Jairus Begs for His Daughter
\p
\v 21 And when Jesus had crossed over again in the boat to the other side, a great crowd gathered to him, and he was beside the sea.
\v 22 And one of the rulers of the synagogue came, Jairus by name \add [i.e. a man of standing, responsible for the order of the assembly]\add*, and when he saw him he fell at his feet
\v 23 and begged him hard, saying, "My little daughter is at the last \add [i.e. she is at the point of death]\add*. Come and lay your hands on her, so that she may be rescued\f + \fr 5:23 \ft Greek \tl sōzō\tl*, which covers both being healed and being saved, and this book plays on the whole range: the woman who touches his clothes (5:28, 34), the crowds at Gennesaret (6:56), blind Bartimaeus (10:52), and then "whoever wants to rescue his life will lose it" (8:35), "then who can be rescued?" (10:26), and the taunt at the cross, "he rescued others; himself he cannot rescue" (15:31).\f* and live."
\v 24 And he went off with him. And a great crowd was following him and pressing in on him.
\s1 A Woman Who Had Been Bleeding Twelve Years
\p
\v 25 And a woman was there who had been in a flow of blood for twelve years\f + \fr 5:25 \ft By Leviticus 15:25-27 a woman with such a discharge is unclean for as long as it lasts, and so is anything she lies on, sits on, or touches, and so is anyone who touches those things. Twelve years of it means twelve years shut out of the assembly, unable to touch another person without passing the uncleanness on. Her trouble is not only in her body.\f*,
\v 26 and had suffered much under many healers and had spent everything she had, and was no better for it but rather had come to be worse.
\v 27 When she heard about Jesus, she came up behind him in the crowd and touched his garment \add [i.e. from behind, unannounced, because by the law she had no business touching anyone at all]\add*,
\v 28 for she was saying, "If I touch even his garments, I will be rescued."
\v 29 And immediately the spring of her blood dried up, and she knew in her body that she was healed of the scourge.
\v 30 And immediately Jesus knew in himself that power had gone out of him, and he turned around in the crowd and said, "Who touched my garments?"
\v 31 And his disciples said to him, "You see the crowd pressing in on you, and you say, 'Who touched me?'" \add [i.e. hundreds are shoving against him, and only one has reached out]\add*
\v 32 And he kept looking around to see the woman who had done this.
\v 33 And the woman, afraid and trembling, knowing what had happened to her, came and fell down before him and told him the whole truth.
\v 34 And he said to her, "Daughter \add [i.e. a word of belonging, said to a woman twelve years shut out]\add*, your trust has rescued you \add [i.e. not the touch itself — the trust that drove her to it]\add*. Go into peace\f + \fr 5:34 \ft The Hebrew blessing behind the phrase is \tl shalom\tl* — not merely the absence of trouble but wholeness, everything in a life set right and holding.\f*, and be whole of your scourge."
\s1 Talitha Koum
\p
\v 35 While he was still speaking, people came from the house of the ruler of the synagogue, saying, "Your daughter has died. Why bother the teacher any further?" \add [i.e. their reasoning is sound: death ends what a healer can do]\add*
\v 36 But Jesus overheard the word being spoken and said to the ruler of the synagogue, "Do not be afraid. Only trust."
\v 37 And he did not let anyone follow along with him except Peter and James and John the brother of James \add [i.e. the three he will take up the mountain and into Gethsemane]\add*.
\v 38 And they came to the house of the ruler of the synagogue, and he saw an uproar, people weeping and wailing loudly \add [i.e. the hired mourners had already started; the household had accepted that it was over]\add*.
\v 39 And he went in and said to them, "Why the uproar, and the weeping? The child has not died but is sleeping \add [i.e. what is final to you is something I can wake her from]\add*."
\v 40 And they laughed him down. But he threw them all out and took the child's father and mother and those with him and went in where the child was.
\v 41 And taking hold of the child's hand\f + \fr 5:41 \ft "Whoever touches the dead body of any person shall be unclean seven days" (Numbers 19:11). This is the third time in this stretch of the book that he lays hold of what the law quarantines — a leper (1:41), a woman in a flow of blood (5:27), and now a corpse — and every time the contamination runs the wrong way. What should defile him is undone instead.\f* he said to her, "Talitha koum," which translated is, "Little girl, I say to you, get up."\f + \fr 5:41 \ft The words are kept in the Aramaic Jesus spoke and then translated, as this book does at each of its most charged moments — \tl Boanerges\tl* (3:17), \tl Ephphatha\tl* (7:34), \tl Abba\tl* (14:36), \tl Eloi, Eloi, lema sabachthani\tl* (15:34). \tl Talitha\tl* is a tender word, "little lamb." And there is no prayer here: he does not ask God to raise her, he tells her to get up.\f*
\v 42 And immediately the girl got up and was walking around, for she was twelve years old \add [i.e. old enough to walk on her own]\add*. And immediately they were beside themselves with a great amazement.
\v 43 And he ordered them strictly that no one should know this, and he said to give her something to eat \add [i.e. after that, his mind is on whether the child has had her supper]\add*.

\c 6
\s1 Rejected in His Own Town
\p
\v 1 And he went out from there and came into his home town, and his disciples followed him.
\v 2 And when the Sabbath came he began to teach in the synagogue, and many who heard him were struck out of themselves, saying, "Where did this man get these things? And what is this wisdom that has been given to him — and such powerful works happening through his hands?
\v 3 Is this not the carpenter, the son of Mary\f + \fr 6:3 \ft "The carpenter" — Greek \tl tektōn\tl*, a builder who works in wood or stone. A man was normally named as the son of his father, so "the son of Mary" is pointed, whatever lies behind it. The whole list is a village reciting what it knows: his trade, his mother, his brothers by name, his sisters living down the road. In a Galilean village that settled who a man was, and here it becomes the reason they cannot receive him.\f* and brother of James and Joses and Judas and Simon? And are his sisters not here with us?" And they were tripped up over him \add [i.e. what they knew about him became the obstacle they fell over]\add*.
\v 4 And Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his home town, and among his relatives, and in his own house."
\v 5 And he could do no powerful work there at all, except that he laid his hands on a few sick people and healed them.\f + \fr 6:5 \ft The writer does not say he chose not to. He says he could not. Set this beside the woman who had nothing left and reached out anyway and was healed (5:34), and the father told to keep believing after the death notice arrived (5:36). Trust is not decoration on the power; it is the condition under which the power operates among people. Refusal does not merely miss the benefit. It shuts the door.\f*
\v 6 And he marveled because of their lack of trust \add [i.e. everywhere else the crowds marvel at him; here he is the one taken aback]\add*. And he went around the villages in a circuit, teaching.
\s1 He Sends the Twelve Out Two by Two
\p
\v 7 And he called the twelve to himself and began to send them out two by two \add [i.e. in pairs, so that what they say stands on two witnesses]\add*, and he gave them authority over the unclean spirits,
\v 8 and he charged them to take nothing for the road except a staff only — no bread, no bag, no copper in the belt —
\v 9 but to wear sandals, and, "Do not put on two tunics."\f + \fr 6:9 \ft Everything permitted is for walking; everything forbidden is for supplying yourself. They are to go out with no way of feeding themselves and no reserve, which leaves them entirely dependent on whoever takes them in. It is the condition Israel lived under in the wilderness, gathering each day's bread and unable to store it (Exodus 16:4-5) — and a wilderness feeding is coming a few paragraphs from here.\f*
\v 10 And he said to them, "Wherever you go into a house, stay there until you leave that place \add [i.e. do not shop around for better lodging once you are taken in]\add*.
\v 11 And whatever place does not receive you and will not hear you, when you go out from there shake off the dust under your feet as a witness against them."\f + \fr 6:11 \ft Jews returning from Gentile territory shook the foreign dust off before re-entering the land. To perform that gesture on the way out of a Jewish town is to say that the town has put itself outside.\f*
\v 12 And they went out and proclaimed that men should turn back,
\v 13 and they threw out many demons and rubbed many sick people with oil \add [i.e. oil was the ordinary medicine of the day, and they used it]\add* and healed them.
\s1 Herod Hears
\p
\v 14 And King Herod\f + \fr 6:14 \ft Herod Antipas, son of Herod the Great, who ruled Galilee and Perea for Rome. He was not in fact a king but a tetrarch, and never obtained the higher title, though his subjects used it. Read what follows and the courtesy title acquires an edge.\f* heard, for his name had become known, and they were saying, "John the Immerser has been raised from among the dead, and that is why these powers are at work in him."
\v 15 But others were saying, "He is Elijah." And others were saying, "A prophet, like one of the prophets."
\v 16 But when Herod heard it he said, "John, whom I beheaded — this man has been raised."
\s1 How John Was Killed
\p
\v 17 For Herod himself had sent and seized John and bound him in prison, because of Herodias the wife of Philip his brother, since he had married her.
\v 18 For John had been saying to Herod, "It is not permitted for you to have your brother's wife."\f + \fr 6:18 \ft Leviticus 18:16 and 20:21 forbid a man to take his brother's wife. John is doing what prophets did — standing in front of a ruler and reading him the law to his face, as Nathan did to David (2 Samuel 12) and Elijah to Ahab (1 Kings 21). The tense says he kept saying it; this was a campaign, not a remark.\f*
\v 19 And Herodias had it in for him and wanted to kill him, and she could not,
\v 20 for Herod feared John, knowing him to be a righteous and holy man, and he kept him safe; and when he heard him he was much at a loss, and yet he heard him gladly \add [i.e. he knew the man was right, could not resolve what to do about it, and went on listening]\add*.
\v 21 And a convenient day came \add [i.e. convenient for Herodias, who had been waiting for one]\add*, when Herod on his birthday made a banquet for his great men and the commanders of a thousand and the first men of Galilee.
\v 22 And his daughter Herodias came in and danced, and she pleased Herod and those reclining with him. The king said to the girl, "Ask me for whatever you want, and I will give it to you."
\v 23 And he swore to her many times, "Whatever you ask me I will give you, up to half my kingdom."\f + \fr 6:23 \ft The words are Ahasuerus's to Esther, twice over (Esther 5:3; 7:2). There a king's rash oath ends in the rescue of a whole people; here it ends with a prophet's head on a serving dish.\f*
\v 24 And she went out and said to her mother, "What shall I ask for?" And she said, "The head of John the Immerser."
\v 25 And she came in immediately, in a hurry, to the king and asked, saying, "I want you to give me, right now, the head of John the Immerser on a platter \add [i.e. on a serving dish, as though it were another course at the banquet]\add*."
\v 26 And the king became deeply grieved, but because of the oaths and those reclining at table he did not want to refuse her \add [i.e. what overrides what he knows is not ignorance but the watching eyes of the men in the room]\add*.
\v 27 And immediately the king sent an executioner and ordered him to bring his head. And he went off and beheaded him in the prison
\v 28 and brought his head on a platter and gave it to the girl, and the girl gave it to her mother.
\v 29 And when his disciples heard, they came and took away his corpse and laid it in a tomb.
\s1 Bread in the Wilderness for Five Thousand
\p
\v 30 And the sent ones gathered together to Jesus and reported to him everything they had done and everything they had taught.
\v 31 And he said to them, "Come away by yourselves to a deserted place, and rest a little." For there were many coming and going, and they had no time even to eat.
\v 32 And they went off in the boat to a deserted place by themselves.
\v 33 And many saw them going and recognized them, and they ran there together on foot from all the cities and got there ahead of them.
\v 34 And he came out and saw a great crowd, and his heart went out to them, because they were like sheep that have no shepherd\f + \fr 6:34 \ft The phrase carries a charge. Moses used it when he asked God to appoint a successor so that the congregation would not be like sheep without a shepherd (Numbers 27:17). Ezekiel used it to indict Israel's leaders, who fed themselves and let the flock scatter — and then God answered, "I myself will search for my sheep... I will feed them in a good pasture... I will set over them one shepherd" (Ezekiel 34:5-6, 11, 14, 23). What happens next in this paragraph is that promise being kept.\f*; and he began to teach them many things \add [i.e. he feeds them with teaching before he feeds them with bread]\add*.
\v 35 And when it was already late in the day, his disciples came to him and said, "This place is deserted, and it is already late.
\v 36 Send them away, so that they can go into the countryside and villages around and buy themselves something to eat."
\v 37 But he answered them, "You give them something to eat." And they said to him, "Shall we go and buy two hundred denarii worth of bread and give it to them to eat?"\f + \fr 6:37 \ft A denarius was a day's wage for a laborer, so this is something like eight months' pay. The question is meant as a demonstration that the thing cannot be done.\f*
\v 38 And he said to them, "How many loaves do you have? Go and see." And when they had found out they said, "Five — and two fish."
\v 39 And he ordered them all to recline in groups, group by group, on the green grass \add [i.e. lying down to a proper meal, not snatching food standing up]\add*.
\v 40 And they lay down in garden plots, plot by plot, by hundreds and by fifties.\f + \fr 6:40 \ft Israel in the wilderness was organized under leaders of thousands, hundreds, fifties, and tens (Exodus 18:21, 25). The crowd is being arranged the way the nation was arranged in the desert. And the green grass is not scenery: "he makes me lie down in green pastures... he prepares a table before me" (Psalm 23:2, 5). The shepherd of the previous paragraph is doing what the shepherd of the psalm does.\f*
\v 41 And he took the five loaves and the two fish, looked up into the sky, spoke the blessing, and broke the loaves and kept giving them to his disciples to set before the people; and he divided the two fish among them all.\f + \fr 6:41 \ft Took, blessed, broke, gave. The same four verbs in the same order will be used at the table on the last night of his life (14:22). A reader who reaches that room will hear this hillside in it.\f*
\v 42 And they all ate and were filled,
\v 43 and they picked up twelve baskets full of broken pieces, and of the fish.\f + \fr 6:43 \ft Greek \tl kophinos\tl*, the stiff wicker basket that Roman writers treated as a mark of a Jew on the road. Twelve of them, for the twelve tribes. When the crowd is fed on the Gentile side of the lake, the leftovers will be counted in a different kind of basket (8:8), and he will make the disciples repeat both numbers back to him (8:19-20).\f*
\v 44 And those who ate the loaves were five thousand men.
\s1 He Walks on the Sea
\p
\v 45 And immediately he made his disciples get into the boat and go on ahead to the other side, to Bethsaida, while he himself sent the crowd away \add [i.e. he compels them into the boat; a crowd fed in the desert and drawn up by hundreds and fifties is a dangerous thing to leave standing]\add*.
\v 46 And when he had said goodbye to them he went off to the mountain to pray.
\v 47 And when evening came, the boat was in the middle of the sea, and he was alone on the land.
\v 48 And he saw them tormented in their rowing, for the wind was against them. And about the fourth watch of the night \add [i.e. between three and six in the morning; they had been at the oars most of the night]\add* he came to them, walking on the sea\f + \fr 6:48 \ft "He alone stretched out the heavens and walks on the sea as on dry ground" (Job 9:8, Greek text). Job says this to establish that the one he is up against is God. Isaiah says the same of the God who "makes a way through the sea" (Isaiah 43:16), and the psalm says "your way was in the sea, and your path in the mighty waters, and your footprints were not known" (Psalm 77:19).\f*, and he meant to pass by them.\f + \fr 6:48 \ft An odd thing to say if it were about travel arrangements. But "passing by" is the language of God showing himself: "I will make all my goodness pass before you," and then the LORD passes by Moses in the cleft of the rock (Exodus 33:19, 22; 34:6); "the LORD was passing by," said to Elijah on the same mountain (1 Kings 19:11). This is not a rescue that went wrong. It is a self-disclosure, and they do not recognize it.\f*
\v 49 But when they saw him walking on the sea, they thought it was a ghost, and they screamed out;
\v 50 for they all saw him and were shaken. But immediately he spoke with them and said to them, "Take heart — I am. Do not be afraid."\f + \fr 6:50 \ft On the surface it is only "it's me." But the Greek is \tl egō eimi\tl*, the words God speaks in giving his name to Moses at the bush (Exodus 3:14) and repeats through Isaiah — "I am he; before me no god was formed" (Isaiah 43:10; 41:4). Spoken here, by a man walking on water, in the middle of a passing-by, the phrase is doing more work than the disciples can hear. "Do not be afraid" is the standing word at every such appearance (Genesis 15:1; Isaiah 41:10; 43:1).\f*
\v 51 And he went up to them into the boat, and the wind dropped. And they were utterly, beyond all measure, beside themselves,
\v 52 for they had not understood about the loaves, but their heart had been hardened.\f + \fr 6:52 \ft The same word that was used of the men who watched him heal on the Sabbath and went out to arrange his death (3:5). It has now been turned on the twelve. The writer's logic is exact: if they had understood the bread in the desert, a man walking on the sea would not have astonished them. Manna and the sea crossing belong to one story, and it is God's.\f*
\s1 Healings at Gennesaret
\p
\v 53 And when they had crossed over to the land, they came to Gennesaret and moored there.
\v 54 And when they came out of the boat, immediately people recognized him,
\v 55 and they ran around that whole region and began to carry the sick about on their mats to wherever they heard he was.
\v 56 And wherever he went, into villages or into cities or into the countryside, they laid the weak in the marketplaces and begged him that they might touch even the fringe of his garment\f + \fr 6:56 \ft The tassel at the corner of the outer garment, worn in obedience to Numbers 15:38-39 as a reminder to keep God's commands. What one desperate woman did in secret in the crowd (5:27-28) has become what a whole countryside does in the open.\f*; and as many as touched him were rescued.

\c 7
\s1 What Really Makes a Man Unclean
\p
\v 1 And the Pharisees gathered to him, and some of the scribes who had come from Jerusalem \add [i.e. a second delegation down from the capital, as in 3:22]\add*,
\v 2 and they saw that some of his disciples were eating their bread with common hands, that is, unwashed.\f + \fr 7:2 \ft "Common" — Greek \tl koinos\tl* — means ritually defiled, not dirty. Nothing about hygiene is at stake, and neither is anything in the written law: the law prescribes washing for priests before service at the sanctuary (Exodus 30:19-21) and after specific defilements, but it nowhere requires ordinary people to wash before a meal. What follows is the writer stopping to explain a custom to readers who have never lived under it.\f*
\v 3 (For the Pharisees and all the Jews do not eat unless they wash their hands with the fist, holding to the tradition of the elders;
\v 4 and when they come from the marketplace they do not eat unless they immerse themselves; and there are many other things they have received to hold — immersions of cups and pitchers and copper vessels and dining couches.)\f + \fr 7:4 \ft The whole system assumes that defilement travels inward, from the outside, by contact — from the market where you brush against Gentiles, from a vessel someone else has handled. That assumption is exactly what Jesus is about to overturn.\f*
\v 5 And the Pharisees and the scribes asked him, "Why do your disciples not walk according to the tradition of the elders \add [i.e. why do they not conduct their lives by it]\add*, but eat their bread with common hands?"
\v 6 And he said to them, "Well did Isaiah prophesy about you hypocrites\f + \fr 7:6 \ft Greek \tl hypokritēs\tl*, an actor — a man delivering a part on a stage. It says nothing yet about whether he believes his lines.\f*, as it is written: 'This people honors me with the lips, but their heart is far away from me;
\v 7 and in vain they revere me, teaching as teachings the commands of men.'\f + \fr 7:7 \ft Isaiah 29:13, in the Greek version, which sharpens the last line to what he needs: human rules are being taught as though they were God's requirements. "In vain" is the flat word — not imperfect worship, empty worship.\f*
\v 8 You have let go of the command of God and you hold to the tradition of men."
\s1 How a Tradition Cancels a Command
\p
\v 9 And he said to them, "Nicely you set aside the command of God, so that you may set up your tradition \add [i.e. said with the edge on it — you have made an art of it]\add*.
\v 10 For Moses said, 'Honor your father and your mother,' and, 'Whoever speaks evil of father or mother, let him come to his end by death.' \add [i.e. Exodus 20:12 and Exodus 21:17 — and honoring parents means keeping them fed]\add*
\v 11 But you say, if a man says to his father or his mother, 'Korban' — that is, a gift — 'whatever you would have gained from me,'\f + \fr 7:11 \ft Hebrew \tl qorban\tl*, the standard word for an offering brought to God (Leviticus 1:2). A man declares that whatever his parents might have had from him is dedicated, and the dedication binds. He keeps the use of it and they get nothing, and the vow now stands in the way even if he changes his mind.\f*
\v 12 you no longer let him do anything for his father or his mother,
\v 13 cancelling the word of God by the tradition you have handed down. And you do many things like this."
\s1 Nothing from Outside Can Defile
\p
\v 14 And he called the crowd to himself again and said to them, "Hear me, all of you, and understand.
\v 15 There is nothing outside a man which, going into him, can make him common; but the things that come out of a man are what make the man common \add [i.e. defilement does not travel inward by contact; it travels outward from the heart]\add*."
\p
\v 17 And when he went into a house away from the crowd, his disciples asked him about the parable.
\v 18 And he said to them, "Are you without understanding too? Do you not see that nothing going into a man from outside can make him common,
\v 19 since it does not go into his heart but into his belly, and goes out into the latrine — cleansing all the foods?"\f + \fr 7:19 \ft The last three words are the writer's own comment on what Jesus has just said, not part of the saying: by this principle, he has declared all foods clean. If nothing entering from outside can defile, then the clean-and-unclean lists of Leviticus 11 and Deuteronomy 14 no longer mark anybody off — and neither does the table that separates Jew from Gentile. For a community with Gentile members, this was the most contested question there was, and the writer settles it from Jesus' own mouth. Watch where the story goes in the very next verse.\f*
\v 20 And he said, "What comes out of a man, that is what makes the man common.
\v 21 For from inside, out of the heart of men, come the evil reasonings: whorings, thefts, murders,
\v 22 adulteries, graspings, wickednesses, deceit, shamelessness, an evil eye \add [i.e. the envy that begrudges another man his good]\add*, blasphemy, arrogance, folly.
\v 23 All these evil things come out from inside and make the man common \add [i.e. not one item on that list has anything to do with hands or food]\add*."
\s1 The Syrophoenician Woman
\p
\v 24 And he got up from there and went off into the region of Tyre \add [i.e. straight out of Jewish territory into a Gentile city, immediately after saying that nothing from outside defiles]\add*. And he went into a house and wanted no one to know, and he could not escape notice.
\v 25 But immediately a woman heard about him whose little daughter had an unclean spirit, and she came and fell down at his feet.
\v 26 Now the woman was a Greek, a Syrophoenician by birth \add [i.e. a Gentile, and a native of that coast]\add*. And she asked him to throw the demon out of her daughter.
\v 27 And he said to her, "Let the children be filled first, for it is not good to take the children's bread and throw it to the little dogs."\f + \fr 7:27 \ft Two words carry the sentence. "First" is not "only": it sets an order, and an order implies what comes after it. And the word for dogs is a diminutive — not the scavenging packs of the street, which was a term of contempt, but the small dogs that live in the house and lie under the table while the family eats. He has drawn a household, with Israel as the children in it and this woman inside the door.\f*
\v 28 But she answered and said to him, "Lord, even the little dogs under the table eat from the children's crumbs."\f + \fr 7:28 \ft She does not argue with the picture; she stands inside it and works it out. She is not asking him to turn from Israel to her. She is asking for the overflow — what falls while the children are being fed, which costs the children nothing. Twelve baskets were left over the last time he fed them (6:43).\f*
\v 29 And he said to her, "Because of this word, go — the demon has gone out of your daughter."
\v 30 And she went off to her house and found the child lying on the bed, and the demon gone out \add [i.e. no command spoken over the girl, no journey, nothing but the exchange of words]\add*.
\s1 Ephphatha
\p
\v 31 And he went out again from the region of Tyre and came through Sidon to the sea of Galilee, through the middle of the region of the Decapolis \add [i.e. a long way around, and every mile of it through Gentile country]\add*.
\v 32 And they brought him a deaf man who could hardly speak\f + \fr 7:32 \ft The Greek word for a man whose speech will not come, \tl mogilalos\tl*, is rare to the point of being unique: it occurs once in the Greek Old Testament, at Isaiah 35:6 — "then the tongue of the speechless will shout for joy," in the passage describing what the world will look like when God comes to save. The word is planted here so that the crowd's shout at the end of the paragraph can be recognized for what it is.\f*, and they begged him to lay his hand on him.
\v 33 And he took him aside from the crowd, by himself, and put his fingers into his ears, and spat, and touched his tongue \add [i.e. sign language for a man who cannot hear: I am dealing with your ears, and with your tongue]\add*.
\v 34 And he looked up into the sky and groaned and said to him, "Ephphatha," that is, "Be opened."
\v 35 And immediately his ears were opened and the bond of his tongue was untied, and he was speaking straight.
\v 36 And he ordered them to tell no one; but the more he ordered them, the more they proclaimed it.
\v 37 And they were struck out of themselves beyond all measure, saying, "He has done everything well. He even makes the deaf hear and the speechless speak."\f + \fr 7:37 \ft Two scriptures at once, and the crowd saying them may not know either. "He has done everything well" is the verdict on the sixth day of creation: "God saw everything that he had made, and it was very good" (Genesis 1:31). And the deaf hearing and the speechless speaking are the marks Isaiah gave for the day God comes to save his people (Isaiah 35:5-6). The men in Jerusalem were arguing about unwashed hands. It is a Gentile crowd in the Decapolis that sees what is happening.\f*

\c 8
\s1 Bread in the Wilderness for Four Thousand
\p
\v 1 In those days, when again there was a great crowd and they had nothing to eat, he called the disciples to himself and said to them,
\v 2 "My heart goes out to the crowd, because they have stayed with me three days already and have nothing to eat.
\v 3 And if I send them off to their houses hungry, they will give out on the road; and some of them have come from far off."
\v 4 And his disciples answered him, "Where could anyone get bread to fill these people, out here in a wilderness?" \add [i.e. word for word the objection they raised the last time, when he fed five thousand]\add*
\v 5 And he asked them, "How many loaves do you have?" And they said, "Seven."
\v 6 And he told the crowd to lie down on the ground; and he took the seven loaves, gave thanks, broke them, and kept giving them to his disciples to set before the people, and they set them before the crowd.
\v 7 And they had a few small fish; and he spoke the blessing over them and said to set these before them too.
\v 8 And they ate and were filled, and they picked up seven hampers of leftover pieces.\f + \fr 8:8 \ft A different word for the basket this time — Greek \tl spyris\tl*, a large basket with nothing Jewish about it, where the twelve on the Galilean shore were \tl kophinoi\tl* (6:43). And a different number: seven, the number of fullness. This crowd is on the Gentile side of the lake. The bread has been broken twice now, once for the children and once for those under the table, and both times there was more left than they began with. He will make the disciples recite both sets of numbers back to him in a few verses, and they still will not see it.\f*
\v 9 And there were about four thousand. And he sent them away.
\v 10 And immediately he got into the boat with his disciples and came into the district of Dalmanutha.
\s1 No Sign Will Be Given
\p
\v 11 And the Pharisees came out and began to argue with him, seeking from him a sign from the sky, testing him \add [i.e. the healings and the feedings do not count; they want something from the sky]\add*.
\v 12 And he groaned up from deep in his spirit and said, "Why does this generation\f + \fr 8:12 \ft "This generation" is loaded language. It is what Moses called the people in the desert — "a crooked and perverse generation" (Deuteronomy 32:5, 20) — and what the psalm calls them: "for forty years I loathed that generation" (Psalm 95:10). That generation had seen the sea open and eaten bread from heaven, and still demanded proof. These men have just been handed the same two things.\f* seek a sign? Amen, I say to you, if a sign shall be given to this generation —"\f + \fr 8:12 \ft The sentence breaks off because it is an oath. The Hebrew form runs "if such-and-such happens..." and leaves the curse on oneself unspoken. It is the strongest refusal the language has: no sign, none, ever.\f*
\v 13 And he left them, got into the boat again, and went off to the other side \add [i.e. no argument, no explanation; he simply goes]\add*.
\s1 The Leaven, and the Loaves They Do Not Understand
\p
\v 14 And they had forgotten to take bread, and they had no more than one loaf with them in the boat \add [i.e. they are worrying about bread while sitting beside the man who has twice fed thousands with none]\add*.
\v 15 And he ordered them, saying, "Look out. Watch out for the leaven of the Pharisees and the leaven of Herod."\f + \fr 8:15 \ft Leaven is the standard picture for a small thing that works its way through the whole lump, and at Passover every scrap of it was hunted out of the house (Exodus 12:15-20). Two kinds are named. The Pharisees' leaven has just been on display: seeing everything and demanding a sign anyway. Herod's has already been shown at length — a man who knew John was righteous, enjoyed listening to him, and killed him because of who was watching (6:20-26).\f*
\v 16 And they were reasoning with one another that they had no bread.
\v 17 And he knew it and said to them, "Why are you reasoning that you have no bread? Do you still not see or understand? Have you a hardened heart?
\v 18 Having eyes, do you not see? And having ears, do you not hear? And do you not remember?\f + \fr 8:18 \ft The words are the prophets' indictment of a rebellious people: "who have eyes and do not see, who have ears and do not hear" (Jeremiah 5:21; Ezekiel 12:2). And they are his own words from 4:12, where he used them of "those outside." He is now saying them to the twelve. Remembering, in Israel's language, is not recalling a fact but treating what God has already done as the ground for trusting him now; forgetting is the wilderness generation's whole failure (Psalm 78:42).\f*
\v 19 When I broke the five loaves for the five thousand, how many baskets full of broken pieces did you pick up?" They said to him, "Twelve."
\v 20 "When I broke the seven for the four thousand, how many hampers full of pieces did you pick up?" And they said to him, "Seven."
\v 21 And he said to them, "Do you still not understand?" \add [i.e. they have the numbers exactly right and no idea what they mean]\add*
\s1 A Blind Man Who Sees in Two Stages
\p
\v 22 And they came into Bethsaida. And they brought him a blind man and begged him to touch him.
\v 23 And he took hold of the blind man's hand and led him outside the village, and he spat into his eyes and laid his hands on him and asked him, "Do you see anything?"
\v 24 And he looked up and said, "I see the men — I see them like trees, walking." \add [i.e. shapes moving, but nothing in focus]\add*
\v 25 Then he laid his hands on his eyes again, and he looked hard, and he was restored and was seeing everything clearly, right into the distance.\f + \fr 8:25 \ft This is the only healing in the book that takes two attempts, and everything else in it happens at a word. The writer has put it here on purpose, between men who have eyes and cannot see (8:18) and a man who is about to name Jesus correctly and then get the meaning of it exactly wrong (8:29-33). One touch and you see shapes. It takes a second touch to see what you are looking at.\f*
\v 26 And he sent him to his house, saying, "Do not even go into the village."
\s1 You Are the Christ
\p
\v 27 And Jesus and his disciples went out into the villages of Caesarea Philippi \add [i.e. the far northern edge of the land, a town full of shrines to Pan and to Caesar]\add*. And on the road he asked his disciples, "Who do men say that I am?"
\v 28 And they told him, "John the Immerser; and others, Elijah; and others, one of the prophets." \add [i.e. every answer puts him somewhere in the line of the prophets, and every one of them is short]\add*
\v 29 And he asked them, "But you — who do you say that I am?" Peter answered and said to him, "You are the Christ."
\v 30 And he rebuked them, that they should tell no one about him.\f + \fr 8:30 \ft The word is the one used for silencing demons and for shouting down the sea (1:25; 4:39). The answer was right, which makes the reaction strange until you see what the title carried: a king of David's line who would throw out the occupier. Said aloud in that form, in that country, it would mean one thing and start one kind of movement. So he takes the title and immediately fills it with something else.\f*
\s1 The Son of Man Must Suffer
\p
\v 31 And he began to teach them that the son of man must\f + \fr 8:31 \ft "Must" is not a prediction of what is likely. It states a necessity: this is what God has determined, and it is not a route that can be gone around. Everything from here to the end of the book stands on it.\f* suffer much and be rejected after testing \add [i.e. examined by the men whose office is to judge such things, and thrown out]\add* by the elders and the chief priests and the scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise up.
\v 32 And he was speaking the word out in the open \add [i.e. plainly, for the first time — no parable, no secrecy]\add*. And Peter took him aside and began to rebuke him.
\v 33 But he turned around and looked at his disciples and rebuked Peter and said, "Get behind me, Satan, because you are not minding the things of God but the things of men."\f + \fr 8:33 \ft \tl Satan\tl* is the Hebrew word for an adversary, and in the wilderness the adversary offered him exactly this — the throne without the road to it (1:13). "Get behind me" is not dismissal: "come after me" was the call to follow (1:17), and behind is where a follower belongs. Peter has stepped out in front to steer, and is being sent back into line. The turn to face the others says the mistake is not Peter's alone.\f*
\s1 Take Up Your Cross
\p
\v 34 And he called the crowd to himself along with his disciples \add [i.e. what follows is not private counsel for the twelve; anyone may hear it]\add* and said to them, "If anyone wants to come after me, let him deny himself and pick up his cross\f + \fr 8:34 \ft In an occupied province this was not a figure of speech. Crucifixion was Rome's punishment for slaves and rebels, staged in public to terrify everyone watching, and the condemned man carried the crossbeam through the streets to the place of execution. To pick up a cross is to walk out of the town as a man already sentenced.\f* and follow me.
\v 35 For whoever wants to rescue his life\f + \fr 8:35 \ft Greek \tl psychē\tl*, which is not a part of a person but the person alive — the self, the whole life. The saying works on the double sense of "rescue" that runs through the book (see the note at 5:23): save your skin and you lose yourself; lose your skin for his sake and yourself is what you get back.\f* will lose it; but whoever loses his life for my sake and for the good news will rescue it.
\v 36 For what does it profit a man to gain the whole world and forfeit his life?
\v 37 For what could a man give in exchange for his life?\f + \fr 8:37 \ft The language is a merchant's — profit, forfeit, exchange. "No man can redeem his brother or give God a ransom for him; the redemption of his life is costly, and can never suffice" (Psalm 49:7-8). Once it is gone there is no currency that buys it back.\f*
\v 38 For whoever is ashamed of me and of my words in this adulterous\f + \fr 8:38 \ft Not a comment on anyone's sexual conduct. In the prophets, adultery is the standing image for Israel breaking faith with the God she is married to (Hosea 1-3; Ezekiel 16 and 23). He is charging his generation with covenant infidelity.\f* and sinful generation, the son of man will also be ashamed of him when he comes in the glory of his Father with the holy angels \add [i.e. the same Son of Man who must be killed is the one who comes in glory; they are not two figures]\add*."

\c 9
\p
\v 1 And he said to them, "Amen, I say to you, there are some standing here who will not taste death\f + \fr 9:1 \ft "Taste death" is a Semitic way of saying "die." The promise is not that the world ends within their lifetimes but that some of the men listening will see God's reign break out in power before they are gone. Six days later, three of them go up a mountain.\f* until they see the kingdom of God come in power."
\s1 Transfigured on the Mountain
\p
\v 2 And after six days\f + \fr 9:2 \ft This book almost never counts days; it says "immediately" and moves on. A precise count is a signal. "The glory of the LORD settled on Mount Sinai, and the cloud covered it six days, and on the seventh day he called to Moses out of the cloud" (Exodus 24:16).\f* Jesus took Peter and James and John and brought them up a high mountain, by themselves, alone. And he was changed in form in front of them,
\v 3 and his garments became gleaming, exceedingly white, whiter than any cloth-bleacher on earth could make them \add [i.e. not a whiteness anyone could produce; it does not come from here]\add*.
\v 4 And Elijah appeared to them, with Moses, and they were talking with Jesus.\f + \fr 9:4 \ft The two men in Israel's story who met God on this same mountain — Moses at Sinai (Exodus 19-34), Elijah at Horeb, which is the same place (1 Kings 19:8-18) — and the two whose departures from life were not ordinary (Deuteronomy 34:5-6; 2 Kings 2:11). Both were expected back in some form: a prophet like Moses whom Israel must hear (Deuteronomy 18:15-18), and Elijah before the day of the LORD (Malachi 4:5). The Law and the Prophets are standing beside him, and what the writer wants seen is that the man who has just said he must be killed is standing there in glory.\f*
\v 5 And Peter answered and said to Jesus, "Rabbi, it is good for us to be here. Let us make three booths — one for you and one for Moses and one for Elijah." \add [i.e. shelters of branches, to hold the moment in place — and three of them, as though the three were equals]\add*
\v 6 For he did not know what to answer, for they had become terrified.
\v 7 And a cloud came, overshadowing them, and a voice came out of the cloud: "This is my Son, the beloved. Hear him."\f + \fr 9:7 \ft The cloud is where God's presence has always shown itself — on Sinai, filling the tent, filling the temple (Exodus 19:16; 40:34-35; 1 Kings 8:10-11). At the river the voice spoke to Jesus, "You are my Son" (1:11); here it speaks to the three about him. And it adds a command, which is Moses' own about the prophet God would raise up: "to him you shall listen" (Deuteronomy 18:15). Note what they are being told to listen to. The last thing he said was that he must suffer and be killed, and Peter tried to talk him out of it.\f*
\v 8 And suddenly, looking around, they no longer saw anyone but Jesus alone with them \add [i.e. the Law and the Prophets have spoken and withdrawn; what remains is the Son they were told to hear]\add*.
\s1 Elijah Has Already Come
\p
\v 9 And as they were coming down from the mountain he ordered them to tell no one what they had seen, until the son of man should rise from among the dead \add [i.e. a time limit, for once — before the resurrection this would be told as glory without a cross, which is precisely the mistake]\add*.
\v 10 And they held on to the word among themselves, arguing over what rising from among the dead meant.
\v 11 And they asked him, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"\f + \fr 9:11 \ft Malachi 4:5-6: God will send Elijah before the great and terrible day of the LORD, and he will turn the hearts of fathers to their children. The disciples have just seen Elijah, and the timetable no longer makes sense to them.\f*
\v 12 And he said to them, "Elijah does come first and puts everything back as it was. And how is it written of the son of man that he must suffer much and be treated as nothing?\f + \fr 9:12 \ft He grants the scribes their text and asks them for the other one. "I am a worm and not a man, a reproach of men and despised by the people" (Psalm 22:6) — the psalm he will quote from the cross. They teach the half about Elijah and leave out the half about what happens to the one Elijah comes ahead of.\f*
\v 13 But I say to you that Elijah has come, and they did to him whatever they wanted, as it is written of him."\f + \fr 9:13 \ft He is talking about John, who came out of the desert dressed as Elijah (1:6) and whose head ended on a serving dish at a banquet (6:27-28). No single passage narrates a death of Elijah; what is written is the pattern — Israel's messengers are hunted and killed by the people they are sent to (1 Kings 19:2, 10; Nehemiah 9:26; Jeremiah 26:20-23). The forerunner's fate is the plainest statement yet of what waits for the one who follows.\f*
\s1 I Trust — Help My Lack of Trust
\p
\v 14 And when they came to the disciples they saw a great crowd around them, and scribes arguing with them.
\v 15 And immediately, when the whole crowd saw him, they were astonished, and they ran up and greeted him.
\v 16 And he asked them, "What are you arguing with them about?"
\v 17 And one out of the crowd answered him, "Teacher, I brought my son to you. He has a speechless spirit,
\v 18 and wherever it seizes him it throws him down, and he foams and grinds his teeth and goes stiff. And I told your disciples to throw it out, and they were not strong enough \add [i.e. they had been given authority to do exactly this, and it failed in their hands]\add*."
\v 19 And he answered them, "O generation without trust, how long will I be with you? How long will I put up with you? Bring him to me."\f + \fr 9:19 \ft Moses came down from the mountain and found the people making a calf; the man who comes down this mountain finds the same thing in a different form. And the words are close to Moses' own cry: "How long will this people spurn me, and how long will they not believe in me, for all the signs I have done among them?" (Numbers 14:11).\f*
\v 20 And they brought him to him. And when the spirit saw him, immediately it threw the boy into convulsions, and he fell on the ground and rolled about, foaming.
\v 21 And he asked his father, "How long has this been happening to him?" And he said, "From childhood.
\v 22 And it has often thrown him into fire and into water, to destroy him \add [i.e. the aim was never torment; the aim was to kill him]\add*. But if you can do anything, let your heart go out to us and help us."
\v 23 And Jesus said to him, "'If you can'! Everything is possible for the one who trusts."\f + \fr 9:23 \ft He hands the father's word back to him with the doubt moved. The father said, "if you can do anything"; the answer puts the "if" on the other side. This is not the claim that a man's confidence generates power — "all things are possible with God" is how the same phrase is finished at 10:27. Trust is the open hand, not the source.\f*
\v 24 Immediately the father of the child screamed out, "I trust! Help my lack of trust!" \add [i.e. both at once, and he brings the second to Jesus as well as the first]\add*
\v 25 And when Jesus saw that a crowd was running together, he rebuked the unclean spirit, saying to it, "Speechless and deaf spirit, I order you: come out of him and never go into him again \add [i.e. no other exorcism in this book bars the door behind]\add*."
\v 26 And it screamed and convulsed him violently and came out; and he became like a corpse, so that most of them said, "He has died."
\v 27 But Jesus took hold of his hand and raised him, and he stood up.\f + \fr 9:27 \ft Every verb in the sentence belongs to the resurrection vocabulary of this book: he took his hand, as with the dead girl (5:41); he raised him — the word used at 16:6, "he has been raised"; and he stood up, from the root behind "rising from the dead."\f*
\v 28 And when he had gone into a house, his disciples asked him privately, "Why could we not throw it out?"
\v 29 And he said to them, "This kind cannot come out by anything except by prayer."\f + \fr 9:29 \ft Not a stronger technique. Prayer is the posture of a man who has nothing of his own to bring, and the authority they were given was never theirs to carry around apart from the one who gave it. They tried it as a possession and it was not there. Some manuscripts add "and fasting."\f*
\s1 The Second Time He Tells Them He Will Be Killed
\p
\v 30 And they went out from there and were passing through Galilee, and he did not want anyone to know,
\v 31 for he was teaching his disciples and saying to them, "The son of man is being handed over into the hands of men\f + \fr 9:31 \ft In Greek the sentence turns on one word: the son of \tl anthrōpos\tl* is handed into the hands of \tl anthrōpoi\tl*. The one who bears humanity's name is delivered to humanity. And "handed over" is passive — Judas will do it, the priests will do it, Pilate will do it, and behind them all stands the "must" of 8:31.\f*, and they will kill him, and when he has been killed, after three days he will rise."
\v 32 But they did not understand the word, and they were afraid to ask him \add [i.e. they had learned from what happened to Peter not to argue, and learned nothing else]\add*.
\s1 Who Is the Greatest?
\p
\v 33 And they came into Capernaum. And when he was in the house he asked them, "What were you reasoning about on the road?"
\v 34 But they kept quiet, for on the road they had been arguing with one another about who was greatest \add [i.e. he had just told them he was going to be killed, and they were ranking themselves]\add*.
\v 35 And he sat down and called the twelve and said to them, "If anyone wants to be first, he shall be last of all and servant of all."\f + \fr 9:35 \ft Greek \tl diakonos\tl*, the man who waits at the table — not a title of honor but a description of work, and work done under everyone he serves. He does not tell them to stop wanting to be great. He tells them what greatness is.\f*
\v 36 And he took a child and stood it in the middle of them \add [i.e. in the place the greatest would occupy]\add*, and he took it in his arms and said to them,
\v 37 "Whoever receives one of these children on my name \add [i.e. does it as my representative, on my authority]\add* receives me; and whoever receives me does not receive me but the one who sent me."\f + \fr 9:37 \ft A child in that world had no standing, no rights of its own, and nothing to give back — serving one buys you nothing. The chain runs from the lowest person in the room through Jesus to God. That is the route, and there is no other.\f*
\s1 Whoever Is Not Against Us
\p
\v 38 John said to him, "Teacher, we saw someone throwing out demons in your name, and we tried to stop him, because he was not following us." \add [i.e. not "he was not following you" — us]\add*
\v 39 But Jesus said, "Do not stop him. For no one who does a powerful work on my name will be able soon after to speak evil of me.
\v 40 For whoever is not against us is for us \add [i.e. the test is not whether a man belongs to your circle]\add*.
\v 41 For whoever gives you a cup of water to drink in the name that you belong to Christ — amen, I say to you, he will not lose his wages \add [i.e. down to a cup of water, and it counts]\add*.
\s1 Cut Off Whatever Trips You Up
\p
\v 42 And whoever trips up one of these little ones who trust in me, it would be better for him if a donkey's millstone were hung around his neck and he were thrown into the sea.\f + \fr 9:42 \ft The upper stone of a mill too heavy for a man to turn — it took a donkey. And drowning was reckoned among the worst of ends, partly because it left nothing to bury. He has just set a child in the middle of a quarrel about rank. Anyone whose scramble for position brings down one of the powerless would be better off at the bottom of the lake.\f*
\v 43 And if your hand trips you up, cut it off. It is better for you to go into the life maimed than to have two hands and go off into Gehenna\f + \fr 9:43 \ft Ge-Hinnom, the ravine on the south-west side of Jerusalem where kings of Judah had burned their children in sacrifice, until Josiah defiled the place to stop it (2 Kings 23:10). Jeremiah renamed it the Valley of Slaughter and made it the picture of God's judgment (Jeremiah 7:31-32). By this period the name had become the standard word for the end that awaits the wicked. It is a real valley, and everyone listening knew what had been done in it.\f*, into the fire that is not put out.
\v 45 And if your foot trips you up, cut it off. It is better for you to go into the life lame than to have two feet and be thrown into Gehenna.
\v 47 And if your eye trips you up, throw it out. It is better for you to go into the kingdom of God with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into Gehenna,
\v 48 where their worm does not come to an end and the fire is not put out.\f + \fr 9:48 \ft The last verse of Isaiah (66:24), where the rescued go out and look on the corpses of those who rebelled against God: "their worm shall not die, nor shall their fire be quenched." Worm and fire are the two things that consume a dead body. That neither stops means nothing halts the destruction, not that the process runs on for its own sake.\f*
\v 49 For everyone will be salted with fire.\f + \fr 9:49 \ft "Every offering of your grain offering you shall season with salt; you shall not let the salt of the covenant of your God be missing" (Leviticus 2:13). Salt went on everything laid on the altar, and a covenant sealed in salt was a covenant that held (Numbers 18:19). Fire on an offering does not destroy it; it consecrates it. Read that way, the fire in this verse is not the fire of the previous one: it is what tests and makes fit those who take up a cross.\f*
\v 50 Salt is good; but if the salt becomes saltless, with what will you season it? Have salt in yourselves, and be at peace with one another." \add [i.e. and there is the answer to the argument they were having on the road]\add*

\c 10
\s1 What God Has Yoked Together
\p
\v 1 And he got up from there and came into the region of Judea and beyond the Jordan \add [i.e. Herod's territory, where saying the wrong thing about a marriage had already cost John his head]\add*, and crowds came together to him again, and as he was used to doing, he taught them again.
\v 2 And Pharisees came and asked him whether it is permitted for a man to send his wife away, testing him.\f + \fr 10:2 \ft A live and bitter dispute. One school held that a man could divorce only for sexual immorality; the other, that he could divorce for anything at all that displeased him. Whichever way he answers he makes enemies, and he is standing in the jurisdiction of the ruler who executed John for saying his marriage was unlawful (6:17-18).\f*
\v 3 And he answered them, "What did Moses command you?"
\v 4 And they said, "Moses allowed a man to write a document of divorce and send her away."\f + \fr 10:4 \ft Deuteronomy 24:1-4, which does not command divorce or authorize it. It takes the practice as already going on and regulates one consequence of it — a woman who has been passed to a second husband may not be taken back by the first. They are reading a limitation on damage as a permission.\f*
\v 5 And Jesus said to them, "It was for your hardness of heart that he wrote you this command \add [i.e. the ruling was a concession to what you are, not a statement of what God wanted]\add*.
\v 6 But from the beginning of creation, 'male and female he made them.'
\v 7 'For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and will cling to his wife,
\v 8 and the two will be one flesh.' So they are no longer two but one flesh.\f + \fr 10:8 \ft Genesis 1:27 and 2:24. He does not deny what Moses wrote; he puts it in its place. Moses ruled on a broken situation. Genesis states what the thing is for. When the two accounts differ, the older and deeper one governs.\f*
\v 9 What God has yoked together, then, let no man separate \add [i.e. God does the joining; the man who breaks it is undoing God's work, not his own]\add*."
\v 10 And in the house the disciples asked him about this again.
\v 11 And he said to them, "Whoever sends his wife away and marries another commits adultery against her;\f + \fr 10:11 \ft "Against her" overturns the usual reckoning, in which a man could wrong another husband but not his own wife. Here the wife is the party wronged, which assumes she has a standing to be wronged in.\f*
\v 12 and if she sends her husband away and marries another, she commits adultery."\f + \fr 10:12 \ft Under Jewish law a woman could not normally initiate a divorce; under Roman law she could. The even-handed wording fits readers living in the empire, and it holds both parties to the same rule.\f*
\s1 Receiving the Kingdom Like a Child
\p
\v 13 And they were bringing children to him so that he might touch them, but the disciples rebuked them.
\v 14 But when Jesus saw it he was indignant \add [i.e. the only time in this book he is angry at the twelve]\add* and said to them, "Let the children come to me. Do not stop them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these.
\v 15 Amen, I say to you, whoever does not receive the kingdom of God like a child will never go into it."\f + \fr 10:15 \ft A child in that society owned nothing, earned nothing, and had no claim on anyone; whatever it got, it got as a gift. That is the point of comparison, and the next paragraph makes it concrete. The children have nothing and the kingdom is theirs; the man with everything walks away from it.\f*
\v 16 And he took them in his arms and blessed them warmly, laying his hands on them.
\s1 The Man with Many Possessions
\p
\v 17 And as he was going out onto the road, a man ran up and went down on his knees before him and asked him, "Good teacher, what shall I do to inherit the life of the age?"\f + \fr 10:17 \ft "The life of the age" is the life of the age to come — the world God will set right when he takes up his reign. Note the two halves of the question pulling against each other: what shall I \tl do\tl* to \tl inherit\tl*. An inheritance is not earned; it is left to you.\f*
\v 18 And Jesus said to him, "Why do you call me good? No one is good except one — God.
\v 19 You know the commands: 'Do not murder, do not commit adultery, do not steal, do not bear false witness, do not defraud, honor your father and mother.'"\f + \fr 10:19 \ft All from the second half of the ten — the commands about how you treat other people. Not one of the first four, which are about God, is on the list, and the omission is about to matter. "Do not defraud" is not from the ten at all; it is the law against holding back a hired man's wages and squeezing the poor (Leviticus 19:13; Deuteronomy 24:14), and it may be aimed at how this particular man came by what he owns.\f*
\v 20 And he said to him, "Teacher, all these I have guarded from my youth."
\v 21 And Jesus looked straight at him and loved him \add [i.e. before he answers, and before he fails]\add* and said to him, "One thing you lack. Go, sell whatever you have and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me."\f + \fr 10:21 \ft Not a rule laid on everyone — Peter's house in Capernaum stays standing and in use all through this book. It is a diagnosis of one man. The commands he has kept are the ones about his neighbor; the untested one is the first of all, "you shall have no other gods before me" (Exodus 20:3). This is the instrument that finds out whether his money is his god, and it finds out.\f*
\v 22 But his face fell at the word, and he went away grieving, for he had many possessions.
\s1 Who Then Can Be Rescued?
\p
\v 23 And Jesus looked around and said to his disciples, "How hard it will be for those who have money to go into the kingdom of God!"
\v 24 And the disciples were shaken at his words.\f + \fr 10:24 \ft Their shock has a reason behind it. Wealth was widely read as evidence of God's favor, promised to a faithful nation in so many words (Deuteronomy 28:1-14). If the men who plainly have God's blessing are the ones who cannot get in, the whole accounting collapses — which is what their question in verse 26 is about.\f* But Jesus answered again and said to them, "Children, how hard it is to go into the kingdom of God!
\v 25 It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to go into the kingdom of God."\f + \fr 10:25 \ft The largest animal anyone there had seen and the smallest opening anyone there could name. There was no gate in Jerusalem called the Needle's Eye; that explanation is late and it spoils the sentence. He is not saying it is difficult. He is saying it cannot be done — which is exactly what he then says in verse 27.\f*
\v 26 And they were struck out of themselves all the more, saying to one another, "Then who can be rescued?"
\v 27 Jesus looked straight at them and said, "With men it is impossible, but not with God; for all things are possible with God."\f + \fr 10:27 \ft "Is anything too hard for the LORD?" — asked over a barren woman promised a son (Genesis 18:14). Israel's whole memory of God is of impossible things done: a nation out of a dead womb, bread out of the sky, water out of rock, a people out of Egypt. Getting into the kingdom belongs on that list. It is not a thing a man achieves; it is a thing God does.\f*
\s1 We Have Left Everything
\p
\v 28 Peter began to say to him, "Look, we have left everything and have followed you."
\v 29 Jesus said, "Amen, I say to you, there is no one who has left house or brothers or sisters or mother or father or children or fields for my sake and for the sake of the good news
\v 30 who will not receive a hundredfold now, in this time — houses and brothers and sisters and mothers and children and fields, with persecutions \add [i.e. the new household and the persecutions arrive in the same package]\add* — and in the age that is coming, the life of the age.
\v 31 But many who are first will be last, and the last first."
\s1 The Third Time He Tells Them
\p
\v 32 And they were on the road, going up to Jerusalem, and Jesus was walking on ahead of them, and they were shaken, and those following were afraid \add [i.e. something in the way he was walking told them what was at the end of that road]\add*. And he took the twelve aside again and began to tell them what was about to happen to him:
\v 33 "Look, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the son of man will be handed over to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death and hand him over to the nations \add [i.e. to the Romans, who alone could carry out an execution]\add*,
\v 34 and they will mock him and spit on him and flog him and kill him; and after three days he will rise."\f + \fr 10:34 \ft The third telling, and the most detailed; each has added something the last left out. The list of humiliations is the servant's in Isaiah: "I gave my back to those who struck me... I did not hide my face from shame and spitting" (Isaiah 50:6). Every item of it will be carried out in chapter 15 in this order.\f*
\s1 James and John Ask for Thrones
\p
\v 35 And James and John, the sons of Zebedee, came to him and said to him, "Teacher, we want you to do for us whatever we ask you." \add [i.e. a blank cheque, asked for immediately after he described his own execution]\add*
\v 36 And he said to them, "What do you want me to do for you?"
\v 37 And they said to him, "Grant us to sit, one at your right hand and one at your left, in your glory."
\v 38 And Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup\f + \fr 10:38 \ft In the prophets the cup is God's judgment, handed round and drunk to the dregs: "you have drunk from the LORD's hand the cup of his wrath" (Isaiah 51:17); "take this cup of the wine of wrath from my hand and make all the nations drink it" (Jeremiah 25:15). He will pray to have this cup taken from him in the garden (14:36).\f* that I drink, or be immersed with the immersion I am immersed with\f + \fr 10:38 \ft To be put under, and not in a river: "the waters have come up to my neck; I have sunk in deep mire... the flood sweeps over me" (Psalm 69:1-2); "all your breakers and your waves have gone over me" (Psalm 42:7). Going under is what is coming, and he is asking whether they can go under with him.\f*?"
\v 39 And they said to him, "We can." And Jesus said to them, "The cup that I drink you will drink, and with the immersion I am immersed with you will be immersed;
\v 40 but to sit at my right hand or at my left is not mine to give — it is for those for whom it has been prepared \add [i.e. prepared by God, and not open to lobbying]\add*."
\s1 Not So Among You
\p
\v 41 And when the ten heard it, they began to be indignant about James and John \add [i.e. not because the two had misunderstood, but because they had asked first]\add*.
\v 42 And Jesus called them to himself and said to them, "You know that those who are counted as rulers of the nations lord it over them, and their great men throw their authority down on them \add [i.e. you have seen how power works in the empire you live in]\add*.
\v 43 It is not so among you. But whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant,
\v 44 and whoever wants to be first among you shall be slave of all.
\v 45 For even the son of man did not come to be served but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many."\f + \fr 10:45 \ft Two things at once. The Son of Man in Daniel is the figure all peoples and nations serve (Daniel 7:14); this one came to do the serving. And "ransom" — Greek \tl lytron\tl* — is the price paid to get a captive or a slave out, the word used in the Greek scriptures for God buying Israel out of Egypt (Exodus 6:6; Deuteronomy 7:8). "For many" is the language of Isaiah's servant, who "bore the sin of many" and "poured out his life to death" (Isaiah 53:11-12). The preposition means in place of. This is the answer to "who then can be rescued?" — the price is his own life.\f*
\s1 Bartimaeus Follows Him on the Road
\p
\v 46 And they came into Jericho. And as he was going out from Jericho with his disciples and a sizable crowd, the son of Timaeus, Bartimaeus, a blind beggar, was sitting beside the road \add [i.e. beside the road to Jerusalem, not on it]\add*.
\v 47 And when he heard that it was Jesus the Nazarene, he began to scream out and say, "Son of David, Jesus, have mercy on me!"\f + \fr 10:47 \ft The first human being in this book to call him Son of David, and it is a blind beggar on the roadside. The title means the king promised to David's line (2 Samuel 7:12-16), and in the hope of the time he was the one who would clear the occupier out of Jerusalem. The man cannot see and has the thing right; the men who can see have been arguing about seating.\f*
\v 48 And many rebuked him, that he should keep quiet. But he screamed all the more, "Son of David, have mercy on me!"
\v 49 And Jesus stopped and said, "Call him." And they called the blind man and said to him, "Take heart. Get up. He is calling you."
\v 50 And he threw off his cloak, jumped up, and came to Jesus.\f + \fr 10:50 \ft The cloak was a beggar's bed, his shelter, and the thing he spread on the ground to catch coins — very nearly everything he owned. He throws it away to get to Jesus. A few paragraphs back, a man with a great deal could not put any of it down and went away grieving (10:22).\f*
\v 51 And Jesus answered him, "What do you want me to do for you?" \add [i.e. word for word what he asked James and John]\add* And the blind man said to him, "Rabbouni, that I may see again."
\v 52 And Jesus said to him, "Go. Your trust has rescued you." And immediately he saw again, and he followed him on the road \add [i.e. told to go, and instead he joins the column headed for Jerusalem]\add*.

\c 11
\s1 He Rides into Jerusalem
\p
\v 1 And when they came near to Jerusalem, to Bethphage and Bethany at the Mount of Olives\f + \fr 11:1 \ft The ridge east of the city, looking straight down on the temple. Ezekiel watched the glory of the LORD leave the temple and come to rest on that mountain before the city fell (Ezekiel 11:23), and Zechariah said that on the day of his coming the LORD's feet would stand on it (Zechariah 14:4). The approach route is chosen.\f*, he sent two of his disciples
\v 2 and said to them, "Go into the village opposite you, and immediately as you go into it you will find a colt tied, on which no man has ever yet sat\f + \fr 11:2 \ft An animal that has never been put to work is what the law requires for sacred use (Numbers 19:2; Deuteronomy 21:3). And a king riding a donkey rather than a warhorse is a specific promise: "your king comes to you, righteous and bringing salvation, humble and mounted on a donkey, on a colt" — and the next line says he will cut off the chariot and the war horse and the battle bow (Zechariah 9:9-10). The animal announces what kind of king is arriving.\f*. Untie it and bring it.
\v 3 And if anyone says to you, 'Why are you doing this?' say, 'The Lord has need of it, and he will send it back here immediately.'"
\v 4 And they went off and found a colt tied at a door outside, on the street, and they untied it.
\v 5 And some of those standing there said to them, "What are you doing, untying the colt?"
\v 6 And they told them just what Jesus had said, and they let them go \add [i.e. everything exactly as he said it would be — this entry is staged, not stumbled into]\add*.
\v 7 And they brought the colt to Jesus and threw their cloaks on it, and he sat on it.
\v 8 And many spread their cloaks on the road\f + \fr 11:8 \ft What men did when Jehu was proclaimed king: "they each took his garment and put it under him on the bare steps, and blew the trumpet and said, Jehu is king" (2 Kings 9:13). The crowd is performing a coronation.\f*, and others spread leafy branches they had cut from the fields.
\v 9 And those going ahead and those following were screaming out, "Hosanna! Blessed is the one who comes in the name of the Lord!\f + \fr 11:9 \ft Psalm 118:25-26. "Hosanna" is the Hebrew of verse 25 left untranslated: \tl hoshi'a na\tl*, "save us now." The psalm was sung by pilgrims coming up to the temple gates, and it is the psalm Jesus will quote against the temple authorities two days later — "the stone the builders rejected has become the head of the corner" (Psalm 118:22; Mark 12:10).\f*
\v 10 Blessed is the coming kingdom of our father David! Hosanna in the highest places!" \add [i.e. this line is the crowd's own addition, and it is their reading of what is happening: David's throne is being restored]\add*
\v 11 And he went into Jerusalem, into the temple, and looked around at everything \add [i.e. he inspected it, and did nothing else that day]\add*; and since the hour was already late, he went out to Bethany with the twelve.
\s1 A Fig Tree with Leaves and No Fruit
\p
\v 12 And the next day, when they had come out from Bethany, he was hungry.
\v 13 And he saw from far off a fig tree that had leaves, and he came to see whether he might find anything on it; and when he came to it he found nothing but leaves, for it was not the season for figs.\f + \fr 11:13 \ft The writer tells us plainly that figs were out of season, which rules out reading this as a hungry man's disappointment. It is a prophet's acted sign, of the kind Jeremiah and Ezekiel performed. The fig tree stands for Israel throughout the prophets, and always in this connection: "like the first fruit on the fig tree I saw your fathers" — and then "Ephraim is stricken, their root is dried up, they shall bear no fruit" (Hosea 9:10, 16); "no figs on the fig tree, and the leaves are withered" (Jeremiah 8:13); "there is no first-ripe fig for which I hunger" (Micah 7:1). A tree in full leaf that yields nothing. He has just walked through the temple and looked at everything in it.\f*
\v 14 And he answered and said to it, "May no one ever eat fruit from you again, into the age." And his disciples were listening.
\s1 He Shuts Down the Temple Court
\p
\v 15 And they came into Jerusalem. And he went into the temple and began to throw out those selling and those buying in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those selling doves\f + \fr 11:15 \ft The trade was not corrupt in its origins. The temple tax had to be paid in one particular coinage, so money had to be changed; an animal for sacrifice had to pass inspection, so it was safer to buy one already approved. What matters is where all this was happening: in the Court of the Gentiles, the outer court, which was the only part of the complex a non-Jew was allowed to enter, and therefore the only place a Gentile could pray.\f*,
\v 16 and he would not let anyone carry a vessel through the temple \add [i.e. people had been using the courts as a shortcut across the city]\add*.
\v 17 And he was teaching, and he said to them, "Is it not written, 'My house will be called a house of prayer for all the nations'? But you have made it a cave of bandits."\f + \fr 11:17 \ft Two texts, and both need their surroundings. "A house of prayer for all peoples" comes from Isaiah 56:6-7, a promise that foreigners who join themselves to the LORD will be brought to his holy mountain and their offerings accepted — the very court now filled with tables. And "den of robbers" is Jeremiah 7:11, from the sermon Jeremiah preached standing in the temple gate, to people who stole and murdered and then took shelter in the building and said they were safe. God's answer there was to point them to Shiloh, the sanctuary he had already destroyed once, and to say he would do the same to this one (Jeremiah 7:12-14). This is not a plan to clean the temple up. It is a sentence passed on it.\f*
\v 18 And the chief priests and the scribes heard, and they were looking for how they might destroy him; for they feared him, because the whole crowd was struck out of themselves at his teaching.
\v 19 And whenever evening came, they went out of the city.
\s1 The Fig Tree Withered
\p
\v 20 And passing by early in the morning, they saw the fig tree withered from the roots \add [i.e. dead from the roots up — not pruned, finished]\add*.
\v 21 And Peter remembered and said to him, "Rabbi, look — the fig tree you cursed has withered."
\v 22 And Jesus answered them, "Have the faith of God.
\v 23 Amen, I say to you, whoever says to this mountain\f + \fr 11:23 \ft He is standing on the Mount of Olives or on the road below it, and the mountain in front of them is the one the temple stands on. In the prophets a mountain is an obstacle or a power, and God is the one who levels it: "what are you, great mountain? Before Zerubbabel you shall become a plain" (Zechariah 4:7). The question hanging in the air after a withered fig tree and a condemned temple is where a man goes now to pray, and this is the beginning of the answer.\f*, 'Be lifted up and thrown into the sea,' and does not waver in his heart but trusts that what he says is happening — it will be his.
\v 24 For this reason I say to you, everything you pray for and ask — trust that you have received it, and it will be yours.
\v 25 And whenever you stand praying, forgive, if you have anything against anyone, so that your Father in the heavens may also forgive you your trespasses."\f + \fr 11:25 \ft Notice what is not in this sentence. Forgiveness came through sacrifice, offered by priests, in the building he has just condemned. Here it runs directly between a man, the people he has to forgive, and his Father in the heavens. No altar, no priest, no temple. What was cursed is not simply removed; something is put in its place.\f*
\s1 By What Authority?
\p
\v 27 And they came again into Jerusalem. And as he was walking in the temple, the chief priests and the scribes and the elders \add [i.e. the three groups that make up the ruling council; this is an official inquiry]\add* came to him
\v 28 and said to him, "By what authority are you doing these things? Or who gave you this authority to do these things?"
\v 29 And Jesus said to them, "I will ask you one word; answer me, and I will tell you by what authority I do these things.
\v 30 The immersion of John — was it from heaven or from men? Answer me."\f + \fr 11:30 \ft Not a dodge. John is where this book begins, and John pointed at the one coming after him. If John's commission came from God, then so does everything that followed from it, and the question they asked has already been answered. Their problem is that they never accepted John either, and the crowd did.\f*
\v 31 And they were reasoning among themselves, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Then why did you not trust him?'
\v 32 But shall we say, 'From men'?" — they feared the crowd, for everyone held that John really was a prophet.
\v 33 And they answered Jesus, "We do not know." \add [i.e. they do know; the writer has just shown us them working it out]\add* And Jesus said to them, "Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things."

\c 12
\s1 The Vineyard and the Tenants
\p
\v 1 And he began to speak to them in parables. "A man planted a vineyard and put a fence around it and dug out a wine vat and built a tower, and he leased it to farmers and went away on a journey.\f + \fr 12:1 \ft Word for word out of Isaiah's song: "my beloved had a vineyard on a fertile hill; he dug it and cleared it of stones and planted it with choice vines, and built a tower in the middle of it, and hewed out a wine vat" (Isaiah 5:1-2). Isaiah then says who the vineyard is: "the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel" (5:7). Everyone listening knew it by the second sentence. But Isaiah's complaint was against the vineyard, which grew bad grapes; this complaint is against the tenants. The vineyard is not the problem. The men running it are.\f*
\v 2 And at the season he sent a slave to the farmers, to take from the farmers some of the fruit of the vineyard.
\v 3 And they took him and beat him and sent him away empty.
\v 4 And again he sent another slave to them; and that one they struck on the head and dishonored.
\v 5 And he sent another; and that one they killed — and many others, some they beat and some they killed.\f + \fr 12:5 \ft The slaves are the prophets, and the escalation is a compression of centuries. Jeremiah was beaten and put in the stocks and lowered into a cistern (Jeremiah 20:2; 38:6); Uriah was hunted down and executed by a king (Jeremiah 26:20-23); Zechariah son of Jehoiada was stoned in the temple court by royal order (2 Chronicles 24:20-22). "They killed your prophets who had warned them" (Nehemiah 9:26).\f*
\v 6 He still had one, a beloved son. He sent him to them last, saying, 'They will respect my son.'\f + \fr 12:6 \ft "Beloved son" is what the voice from heaven has said twice in this book, and about one person (1:11; 9:7). He is telling the men who are planning his death exactly what they are about to do, in a story, in the temple, in front of the crowd. And "last of all" means there is no one after.\f*
\v 7 But those farmers said among themselves, 'This is the heir. Come, let us kill him, and the inheritance will be ours.' \add [i.e. they want the vineyard for themselves — that is what all of it has been for]\add*
\v 8 And they took him and killed him and threw him out of the vineyard \add [i.e. and denied him a burial]\add*.
\v 9 What then will the lord of the vineyard do? He will come and destroy the farmers and give the vineyard to others \add [i.e. the vineyard is not destroyed; the tenancy changes hands]\add*.
\v 10 Have you not even read this scripture: 'The stone the builders rejected after testing, this one has become the head of the corner;
\v 11 this came from the Lord, and it is a marvel in our eyes'?"\f + \fr 12:11 \ft Psalm 118:22-23, out of the psalm the crowd sang over him three days ago (11:9). The builders are the men in charge of the building — these men. They examined a stone, judged it unfit, and threw it on the pile; God put it at the corner, where the whole structure bears on it. Their verdict is not the last one.\f*
\v 12 And they were seeking to seize him, and they feared the crowd, for they knew he had told the parable against them. And they left him and went away.
\s1 Caesar's Things and God's
\p
\v 13 And they sent some of the Pharisees and the Herodians to him, to trap him in a word \add [i.e. the same unlikely pair who first agreed to kill him back in Galilee]\add*.
\v 14 And they came and said to him, "Teacher, we know that you are true and that no one's opinion matters to you, for you do not look at men's faces but teach the way of God on the truth. Is it permitted to give the poll tax to Caesar or not? Shall we give, or shall we not give?"\f + \fr 12:14 \ft The flattery is tactical: having just praised him for not caring what anyone thinks, they make it impossible for him to give a careful answer. The poll tax was the head tax Rome levied directly on the province, and it was the grievance that had set off Judas the Galilean's revolt a generation earlier, on the argument that paying it acknowledged a master other than God. Say pay it, and the crowd is finished with him; say do not, and he can be reported for sedition before nightfall.\f*
\v 15 But he knew their playacting and said to them, "Why are you testing me? Bring me a denarius to look at." \add [i.e. he has to ask them for one]\add*
\v 16 And they brought one. And he said to them, "Whose image is this, and whose inscription?" And they said to him, "Caesar's."\f + \fr 12:16 \ft The coin carried the emperor's head and the words "Tiberius Caesar, son of the divine Augustus, high priest" — an image and a claim to divinity, both offensive, and they had one in their purse.\f*
\v 17 And Jesus said to them, "Give back Caesar's things to Caesar, and God's things to God."\f + \fr 12:17 \ft This sounds at first like a division of the world into two departments, and it is not. Ask what is left over for Caesar once God has what is his: "the earth is the LORD's, and everything in it" (Psalm 24:1). The coin carries Caesar's image, so hand it back to him. And what carries God's image? "Let us make man in our image" (Genesis 1:26-27). Give the coin to Caesar and yourself to God.\f* And they marveled at him.
\s1 God of the Living
\p
\v 18 And Sadducees came to him, who say there is no resurrection, and they asked him,\f + \fr 12:18 \ft The priestly aristocracy that ran the temple. They accepted only the five books of Moses as binding, and since those books say nothing explicit about the dead being raised, they denied it. The question they are about to ask is not a question; it is a set piece designed to make resurrection look absurd.\f*
\v 19 "Teacher, Moses wrote for us that if a man's brother dies and leaves a wife behind and leaves no child, his brother is to take the wife and raise up seed for his brother.\f + \fr 12:19 \ft Deuteronomy 25:5-6. If a man died without a son, his brother was to marry the widow, and the first son of that marriage counted as the dead man's heir, so that his name and his land stayed in the family. The story of Tamar (Genesis 38) and the book of Ruth both turn on it.\f*
\v 20 There were seven brothers. And the first took a wife, and when he died he left no seed.
\v 21 And the second took her, and he died leaving no seed; and the third likewise.
\v 22 And the seven left no seed. Last of all the woman also died.
\v 23 In the resurrection, when they rise, whose wife will she be? For the seven had her as wife." \add [i.e. the whole case rests on the assumption that the age to come is this life carried on unchanged]\add*
\v 24 Jesus said to them, "Is this not why you are wandering astray — because you know neither the scriptures nor the power of God?
\v 25 For when they rise from among the dead, they neither marry nor are given in marriage, but are like angels in the heavens \add [i.e. not that they become angels, but that in this one respect they are like them]\add*.
\v 26 But about the dead being raised — have you not read in the book of Moses, at the bush, how God said to him, 'I am the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob'?\f + \fr 12:26 \ft He argues from the only books they grant — Exodus 3:6, at the burning bush. And the whole weight falls on the tense. God does not say he was their God, back when they were alive. Centuries after they were buried he says "I am." A God who names himself by his bond to particular men is not a God whose partners have ceased to exist.\f*
\v 27 He is not God of the dead but of the living. You are wandering far astray."
\s1 The First Command of All
\p
\v 28 And one of the scribes came up, having heard them arguing, and seeing that he had answered them well, he asked him, "Which command is first of all?" \add [i.e. this one has come to ask, not to trap]\add*
\v 29 Jesus answered, "The first is: 'Hear, O Israel, the Lord our God, the Lord is one;
\v 30 and you shall love the Lord your God out of your whole heart and out of your whole life and out of your whole mind and out of your whole strength.'\f + \fr 12:30 \ft Deuteronomy 6:4-5, the \tl Shema\tl*, recited twice daily by every observant Jew and the nearest thing Israel had to a creed. He quotes the confession as well as the command, because the one is the ground of the other: because God is one, there is no rival to divide the loyalty between, and so the love has to be entire — every "whole" in the sentence is deliberate.\f*
\v 31 The second is this: 'You shall love your neighbor as yourself.' \add [i.e. Leviticus 19:18, and in that same chapter the neighbor includes the foreigner living among you]\add* There is no other command greater than these."
\v 32 And the scribe said to him, "Well said, teacher. You have spoken on the truth: he is one, and there is no other besides him;
\v 33 and to love him out of the whole heart and out of the whole understanding and out of the whole strength, and to love the neighbor as oneself, is more than all the whole burnt offerings and the sacrifices."\f + \fr 12:33 \ft He is standing in the temple, where burnt offerings and sacrifices are the day's business, and he says they come second. He is not inventing this: "to obey is better than sacrifice" (1 Samuel 15:22); "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6); "what does the LORD require of you but to do justice, love kindness, and walk humbly with your God?" (Micah 6:8).\f*
\v 34 And when Jesus saw that he had answered with his mind in place, he said to him, "You are not far from the kingdom of God." \add [i.e. near it, and that is not the same as inside it]\add* And no one dared to question him any more.
\s1 David's Son and David's Lord
\p
\v 35 And Jesus answered as he was teaching in the temple, "How do the scribes say that the Christ is the son of David?
\v 36 David himself said in the Holy Spirit, 'The Lord said to my lord, "Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet."'\f + \fr 12:36 \ft Psalm 110:1. The first "Lord" is God; the second is the one God is speaking to, and David calls him "my lord." A father does not address his own descendant that way. So the scribes' formula is not wrong, only far too small: the Christ stands in David's line and above it, and the throne he is given is at God's right hand — which is not a throne taken by an army. Jesus will quote this same verse to the high priest at his trial (14:62).\f*
\v 37 David himself calls him lord — so how is he his son?" And the great crowd was listening to him gladly.
\s1 Watch Out for the Scribes
\p
\v 38 And in his teaching he said, "Watch out for the scribes, who like to walk about in long robes and to be greeted in the marketplaces
\v 39 and to have the front seats in the synagogues and the first places at banquets,
\v 40 who eat up the houses of widows\f + \fr 12:40 \ft Every item in the list before this one is about being seen: the robe, the greeting in the street, the bench at the front facing the congregation, the best couch at dinner. And this is what the display is laid over. Widows had no legal standing of their own and depended on trustees and legal help; men in this position could bleed an estate dry through fees, or through persuading a widow to pledge what she had to the temple. The Torah these men copied out commands the protection of widows at every turn (Deuteronomy 10:18; 24:17; 27:19), and the prophets condemn those who prey on them (Isaiah 1:17; 10:2).\f* and for a show pray long prayers. These will receive a heavier judgment."
\s1 The Widow's Whole Living
\p
\v 41 And he sat down opposite the treasury and watched how the crowd threw copper into the treasury. And many rich people were throwing in much.
\v 42 And one poor widow came and threw in two lepta, which is a quadrans.\f + \fr 12:42 \ft The smallest coins in circulation — the writer converts them into Roman money for readers who would not know the Jewish coin. Two of them come to about a sixty-fourth of a day's wage.\f*
\v 43 And he called his disciples to himself and said to them, "Amen, I say to you, this poor widow threw in more than all those throwing into the treasury.
\v 44 For they all threw in out of what they had left over, but she out of her lack threw in everything she had — her whole living."\f + \fr 12:44 \ft Read this where it stands. The sentence just before it is about scribes who eat up the houses of widows; the sentence just after it is that not one stone of this building will be left on another (13:2). Here is a widow's house being eaten. He does not say her faith is great and he does not say go and do the same; he states what it cost her, and lets it stand between the accusation and the sentence. She has given her whole life — and given it to an institution he has already called a den of bandits and is about to say will be levelled.\f*

\c 13
\s1 Not One Stone Left on Another
\p
\v 1 And as he was going out of the temple \add [i.e. for the last time in this book; he does not enter it again]\add*, one of his disciples said to him, "Teacher, look — what stones, and what buildings!"
\v 2 And Jesus said to him, "Do you see these great buildings? Not one stone will be left here upon a stone that will not be thrown down."\f + \fr 13:2 \ft Herod's temple was one of the wonders of the ancient world; Josephus describes foundation blocks some forty feet long, and white stone and gold that dazzled anyone looking at it in the sun. The reply is not conditional and offers no way out. It is also not unprecedented: Jeremiah told his generation that God would do to this house what he did to Shiloh (Jeremiah 7:14), and Micah said Zion would be plowed as a field and the temple mount become a wooded hill (Micah 3:12). The Romans levelled it in the year 70.\f*
\s1 When Will These Things Be?
\p
\v 3 And as he was sitting on the Mount of Olives opposite the temple \add [i.e. outside it now, looking back at it from the ridge to the east]\add*, Peter and James and John and Andrew asked him privately,
\v 4 "Tell us, when will these things be? And what will be the sign when all these things are about to be brought to completion?" \add [i.e. they are asking about one thing — the destruction he has just described]\add*
\v 5 And Jesus began to say to them, "Watch out that no one leads you astray.
\v 6 Many will come on my name, saying, 'I am,' and they will lead many astray \add [i.e. claiming his own name and his own place]\add*.
\v 7 And when you hear of wars and reports of wars, do not be alarmed. It must happen, but the end is not yet.
\v 8 For nation will rise up against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be earthquakes in place after place; there will be famines. These are the beginning of birth pangs.\f + \fr 13:8 \ft He is listing what is not the sign. Wars, earthquakes, famines — none of it means the end has come, and none of it is to frighten them. "Birth pangs" is the prophets' image for the agony that precedes a deliverance (Isaiah 26:17; Jeremiah 22:23): real pain, and pain that is producing something.\f*
\s1 What Will Happen to You
\p
\v 9 But watch yourselves. They will hand you over to councils, and you will be beaten in synagogues, and you will be made to stand before governors and kings for my sake, as a witness to them.\f + \fr 13:9 \ft The pronoun changes here from what they will see to what will be done to them. And the verb is the one that has followed Jesus through the whole book — handed over, as John was handed over (1:14) and as the Son of Man will be (9:31; 10:33). What happened to the prophets, and to John, and is about to happen to him, is what happens to those who follow him. The courts named are both Jewish and Roman: there is no side to run to.\f*
\v 10 And the good news must first be proclaimed to all the nations.\f + \fr 13:10 \ft The same phrase he used of the temple's purpose — a house of prayer for all the nations (11:17). The temple did not do it. This will be done instead by a persecuted community carrying an announcement, and it "must" be done, on the same divine necessity that stands over his own death (8:31).\f*
\v 11 And when they lead you away and hand you over, do not worry beforehand about what to say, but whatever is given you in that hour, say that; for it is not you speaking but the Holy Spirit.
\v 12 And brother will hand over brother to death, and a father his child, and children will rise up against parents and put them to death.\f + \fr 13:12 \ft Micah's picture of a society coming apart at the joints: "the son treats the father with contempt, the daughter rises against her mother... a man's enemies are the men of his own house" (Micah 7:6).\f*
\v 13 And you will be hated by all because of my name. But the one who endures to the end, this one will be rescued.\f + \fr 13:13 \ft Not the one who starts. The condition is holding out through the whole of it. Set it beside the seed on rocky ground, which took the word with joy and had no root, so that the first pressure finished it (4:16-17). Endurance is what root looks like from outside.\f*
\s1 The Sign, and the Order to Run
\p
\v 14 But when you see the abomination of desolation\f + \fr 13:14 \ft The phrase is Daniel's (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11), and its first fulfilment was within living memory of the writings that discuss it: in 167 BC Antiochus IV stopped the daily sacrifice, set up an altar to Zeus on the altar of burnt offering, and had swine slaughtered there (1 Maccabees 1:54-59). The Greek participle "standing" is masculine where the noun is neuter, which points to a person rather than an object — someone standing where he has no business standing, as Antiochus did.\f* standing where he ought not — let the reader understand\f + \fr 13:14 \ft An aside spoken past the four men on the mountain to whoever is holding the book. The writer will not spell out what he means, which suggests both that his readers can work it out and that putting it in writing would be dangerous.\f* — then let those in Judea flee to the mountains.\f + \fr 13:14 \ft This is the break with everything the memory of Antiochus would lead them to expect. When the sanctuary was profaned before, Israel rose and fought and won it back, and kept the anniversary of its rededication ever after. This time the instruction is to get out. The temple is not going to be saved.\f*
\v 15 Let the one on the roof not come down or go in to take anything out of his house,
\v 16 and let the one in the field not turn back to get his cloak \add [i.e. there will be no time to fetch anything, not even the coat a man sleeps under]\add*.
\v 17 Woe to those who have a child in the belly and to those nursing in those days! \add [i.e. because they cannot run]\add*
\v 18 And pray that it may not happen in winter \add [i.e. when the rains fill the dry streambeds and the roads become impassable]\add*.
\v 19 For those days will be a pressure such as has not happened from the beginning of the creation that God created until now — and never will be.\f + \fr 13:19 \ft Daniel 12:1: "there shall be a time of trouble, such as never has been since there was a nation till that time." He stretches the comparison wider than Daniel did — not since there was a nation, but since there was a creation.\f*
\v 20 And if the Lord had not cut those days short, no flesh would be rescued; but for the sake of the chosen ones, whom he chose, he cut the days short.\f + \fr 13:20 \ft "The chosen ones" is Israel's own word for herself — those God picked out for a purpose (Deuteronomy 7:6; Isaiah 45:4). Note what is and is not said. They are not lifted out of the disaster; they are in it, and its length is shortened on their account. Note also that in this same discourse they are told they must endure to the end (13:13) and warned that the deception is aimed at them (13:22).\f*
\v 21 And then, if anyone says to you, 'Look, here is the Christ,' 'Look, there,' do not trust it.
\v 22 For false christs and false prophets will rise up and will give signs and wonders to lead astray, if possible, the chosen ones.\f + \fr 13:22 \ft "If possible" acknowledges that it is possible; a warning against a danger that could not touch them would be pointless. And the signs are not said to be fakes. What guards them is not that they cannot be deceived but that they have been told in advance — which is what the next verse says.\f*
\v 23 But you, watch out. I have told you everything beforehand.
\s1 The Son of Man Vindicated
\p
\v 24 But in those days, after that pressure, the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light,
\v 25 and the stars will be falling out of the sky, and the powers in the heavens will be shaken.\f + \fr 13:25 \ft This language has a track record, and it is worth knowing what the prophets did with it. "The stars of the heavens will not give their light; the sun will be dark at its rising" — that is the oracle against Babylon (Isaiah 13:10), a city destroyed by an army. "All the host of heaven shall rot away, and the skies roll up like a scroll" — the oracle against Edom (Isaiah 34:4). Joel uses it for the day of the LORD (Joel 2:10, 31). Every time, it describes God bringing down a power that looked as fixed as the sky. The world at the centre of which this temple stands is coming down.\f*
\v 26 And then they will see the son of man coming in clouds with great power and glory.\f + \fr 13:26 \ft Daniel 7:13-14, and the direction of travel there is worth noticing: "with the clouds of heaven one like a son of man was coming, and he came to the Ancient of Days and was presented before him," and was given dominion over all peoples. He is not coming down; he is being brought up and enthroned. The men who condemn him will see the proof of who holds the authority — and what they will see is their own temple in ruins.\f*
\v 27 And then he will send out the angels and gather together his chosen ones from the four winds, from the end of the earth to the end of the sky.\f + \fr 13:27 \ft The gathering promises, word for word out of the prophets: "he will gather you again from all the peoples... if your outcasts are at the ends of the heavens, from there the LORD your God will gather you" (Deuteronomy 30:3-4); "I will bring your offspring from the east and gather you from the west" (Isaiah 43:5-6). The word for "angels" simply means those sent. After the temple falls, the scattered are gathered — and verse 10 has already said how.\f*
\s1 Learn from the Fig Tree
\p
\v 28 Learn the parable from the fig tree: when its branch has already become tender and puts out its leaves, you know that summer is near.
\v 29 So also you, when you see these things happening, know that he is near, at the doors.
\v 30 Amen, I say to you, this generation will not pass away until all these things happen.\f + \fr 13:30 \ft "Generation" means what it has meant every other time he has used it in this book — the people alive and listening (8:12, 38; 9:19). Not a race and not a type of person. The men in front of him will not all be dead before these things take place.\f*
\v 31 The sky and the earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away.
\s1 No One Knows the Day
\p
\v 32 But about that day or the hour no one knows, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son — only the Father.\f + \fr 13:32 \ft A ladder of ignorance with the Son on it. He has just said that this generation will see it, and now says that the day and the hour are not his to know. Authority and knowledge turn out not to be the same thing: he is given the dominion of Daniel 7, and the timing stays with the Father. The practical consequence is in the next four verses — stop calculating and stay awake.\f*
\v 33 Watch out. Stay awake. For you do not know when the time is.
\v 34 It is like a man away from home, who left his house and gave his slaves the authority — to each his work — and commanded the doorkeeper to keep awake.
\v 35 Keep awake, then; for you do not know when the lord of the house is coming — in the evening or at midnight or at cockcrow or early in the morning —\f + \fr 13:35 \ft The four Roman watches of the night, from six in the evening to six in the morning; cockcrow is the third, roughly midnight to three. Hold on to that third watch. Within a day Peter, who is sitting here listening, will fail to stay awake for one hour in a garden and will deny knowing him before the cock has crowed twice (14:37, 72).\f*
\v 36 so that when he comes suddenly he does not find you sleeping.
\v 37 And what I say to you, I say to all: keep awake."

\c 14
\s1 Two Days Before the Passover
\p
\v 1 Now the Passover and the Unleavened Bread\f + \fr 14:1 \ft The feast of Israel's deliverance from Egypt, when a lamb was killed and its blood on the doorposts turned the destroyer aside (Exodus 12). Every scene from here to the cross is set inside it, and the writer expects it to be heard that way.\f* were two days away. And the chief priests and the scribes were looking for how they might seize him by deceit and kill him;
\v 2 for they were saying, "Not during the feast, or there will be an uproar of the people." \add [i.e. their plan was to wait — and events will not let them]\add*
\s1 A Woman Anoints Him for Burial
\p
\v 3 And while he was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper, as he was reclining at table, a woman came who had an alabaster jar of perfumed oil, pure nard, very costly. She broke the jar and poured it down over his head.\f + \fr 14:3 \ft Nard was imported from the mountains of India and priced accordingly; alabaster was used for such oils because it held the scent. She breaks the jar, which means the whole of it goes out at once and nothing is kept back. And pouring oil over a man's head is not a courtesy — it is how a king is installed. Samuel poured it on Saul's head and on David's (1 Samuel 10:1; 16:13), and "Christ" means the man that has been done to. An unnamed woman does it in a private house, two days before the priests kill him.\f*
\v 4 But some were indignant among themselves: "Why has this perfumed oil been wasted like this?
\v 5 For this oil could have been sold for more than three hundred denarii \add [i.e. close to a year's wages]\add* and given to the poor." And they were scolding her harshly.
\v 6 But Jesus said, "Leave her alone. Why do you make trouble for her? She has worked a good work on me.
\v 7 For you always have the poor with you, and whenever you want you can do good for them; but you do not always have me.\f + \fr 14:7 \ft He is quoting a command, not waving one away: "the poor will never cease out of the land, therefore I command you, open your hand to your brother, to the needy and the poor" (Deuteronomy 15:11). The obligation stands and will always be available. What will not always be available is him.\f*
\v 8 She did what she had. She took it in hand beforehand to anoint my body for the burial. \add [i.e. no one else at that table has grasped that there is a burial coming]\add*
\v 9 And amen, I say to you, wherever the good news is proclaimed in the whole world, what she did will also be told, in remembrance of her."
\s1 Judas Goes to the Chief Priests
\p
\v 10 And Judas Iscariot, the one of the twelve, went off to the chief priests to hand him over to them.
\v 11 And when they heard it they were glad and promised to give him silver. And he was looking for how he might hand him over at a good moment. \add [i.e. set beside the woman who poured out everything she had, a man of the inner circle sells him]\add*
\s1 Getting the Passover Ready
\p
\v 12 And on the first day of the Unleavened Bread, when they were slaughtering the Passover lamb\f + \fr 14:12 \ft The lambs were killed in the temple on the afternoon of the fourteenth of Nisan and eaten that evening, since the day turned at sunset. The writer sets his clock by the killing of the lambs.\f*, his disciples said to him, "Where do you want us to go and get ready for you to eat the Passover?"
\v 13 And he sent two of his disciples and said to them, "Go into the city, and a man carrying a jar of water will meet you \add [i.e. a man doing a woman's work, and so easy to pick out in a crowd]\add*. Follow him,
\v 14 and wherever he goes in, say to the master of the house, 'The teacher says, "Where is my guest room, where I may eat the Passover with my disciples?"'
\v 15 And he will show you a large upper room, furnished and ready. Get it ready for us there."
\v 16 And the disciples went out and came into the city and found it just as he had told them, and they got the Passover ready \add [i.e. arranged in advance, as the colt was — he is not being overtaken by these events]\add*.
\s1 One of You Will Hand Me Over
\p
\v 17 And when evening came, he came with the twelve.
\v 18 And as they were reclining and eating, Jesus said, "Amen, I say to you, one of you will hand me over — one who is eating with me."\f + \fr 14:18 \ft To share a meal was to be bound to the man you shared it with; betraying him afterwards was the worst thing a person could do short of murder. "Even my close friend in whom I trusted, who ate my bread, has lifted his heel against me" (Psalm 41:9).\f*
\v 19 They began to grieve and to say to him one by one, "Not I?" \add [i.e. not one of them points at anyone else, and not one of them is sure of himself]\add*
\v 20 And he said to them, "One of the twelve, the one dipping with me into the bowl.
\v 21 For the son of man goes as it is written of him; but woe to that man through whom the son of man is handed over. It would have been good for that man if he had not been born."\f + \fr 14:21 \ft Two things are said in one breath and neither is softened to make room for the other: this is happening as the scriptures said it would, and the man who does it is answerable for it. The writer sets them side by side and does not explain how they fit.\f*
\s1 This Is My Body, This Is My Blood of the Covenant
\p
\v 22 And as they were eating, he took bread, spoke the blessing, broke it, and gave it to them and said, "Take. This is my body."\f + \fr 14:22 \ft The four verbs are the ones from the hillsides where he fed the crowds (6:41; 8:6). And the Passover meal already worked this way: the head of the household lifted each element and said what it stood for — "this is the bread of affliction our fathers ate in Egypt." He follows the form exactly and changes what it points to.\f*
\v 23 And he took a cup, gave thanks, and gave it to them, and they all drank from it.
\v 24 And he said to them, "This is my blood of the covenant, which is poured out for many.\f + \fr 14:24 \ft "Behold the blood of the covenant that the LORD has made with you" — Moses' words at Sinai as he threw the blood of the sacrifices over the people and the covenant took effect (Exodus 24:8). What was sealed there in the blood of oxen is being sealed here in his own. "For many" carries Isaiah's servant with it, who "poured out his life to death" and "bore the sin of many" (Isaiah 53:12), and answers his own words at 10:45 — a ransom for many. Zechariah adds one more line worth having: "because of the blood of my covenant with you, I will set your prisoners free from the waterless pit" (Zechariah 9:11).\f*
\v 25 Amen, I say to you, I will never again drink of the fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it new in the kingdom of God."\f + \fr 14:25 \ft The Passover always ended looking forward. He is taking a vow: he sets the cup down here and does not lift it again. Wine will be offered to him twice on the cross and he takes neither (15:23, 36).\f*
\v 26 And when they had sung the hymn, they went out to the Mount of Olives.\f + \fr 14:26 \ft The Hallel, Psalms 113-118, sung at the close of the Passover meal. So the last thing they sing together includes "the LORD is on my side, I will not fear; what can man do to me?" and "I shall not die but live" and "the stone the builders rejected has become the head of the corner" (Psalm 118:6, 17, 22).\f*
\s1 You Will All Be Tripped Up
\p
\v 27 And Jesus said to them, "You will all be tripped up, for it is written: 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered.'\f + \fr 14:27 \ft Zechariah 13:7, and the full verse is "Awake, sword, against my shepherd, against the man who stands next to me, says the LORD of hosts." Two things in it: the shepherd is God's own intimate, and the striking is God's doing. And "tripped up" is the same word as the rocky ground in the parable (4:17). He is telling the twelve which soil they are about to prove to be.\f*
\v 28 But after I am raised, I will go ahead of you into Galilee." \add [i.e. one sentence of hope in the middle of it, and it is the sentence the messenger will repeat at the empty tomb]\add*
\v 29 But Peter said to him, "Even if they are all tripped up, I will not be."
\v 30 And Jesus said to him, "Amen, I say to you, today, this very night, before the cock crows twice, you will deny me three times."
\v 31 But he kept saying all the more, "Even if I have to die with you, I will never deny you." And they all said the same.
\s1 Gethsemane
\p
\v 32 And they came to a place whose name was Gethsemane \add [i.e. the name means oil press]\add*, and he said to his disciples, "Sit here while I pray."
\v 33 And he took Peter and James and John with him, and he began to be appalled and in anguish \add [i.e. the same three who saw him in glory on the mountain now see this]\add*.
\v 34 And he said to them, "My life within me is crushed with grief, to the point of death. Stay here and keep awake."\f + \fr 14:34 \ft The words come out of the psalms of a man at the end of himself — "why are you cast down, my soul?" (Psalm 42:5); "the cords of death encompassed me... I suffered distress and anguish" (Psalm 116:3). Psalm 116 is one of the Hallel psalms he has just finished singing. And "keep awake" is the very word he pressed on them in the discourse two days ago (13:35-37).\f*
\v 35 And he went forward a little and fell on the ground and was praying that, if it were possible, the hour might pass from him.
\v 36 And he was saying, "Abba, Father\f + \fr 14:36 \ft \tl Abba\tl* is Aramaic for father, the ordinary word a child uses in the house, kept here in the language he actually spoke. Beside it stands "all things are possible for you" — intimacy and sovereignty in one address.\f*, all things are possible for you. Take this cup away from me. But not what I want — what you want."\f + \fr 14:36 \ft The cup is the one he named to James and John (10:38), and in the prophets it is the cup of God's judgment, drunk to the dregs (Isaiah 51:17; Jeremiah 25:15). Two things are true in this sentence and the writer lets both stand: he does not want it, and he takes it. There is nothing performed about this.\f*
\v 37 And he came and found them sleeping, and he said to Peter, "Simon \add [i.e. not Peter, the name he gave him]\add*, are you sleeping? Were you not strong enough to keep awake for one hour?
\v 38 Keep awake and pray, so that you do not come into testing. The spirit is eager, but the flesh is weak."\f + \fr 14:38 \ft "Testing" is the word used for Satan's testing in the wilderness (1:13) and for every trap the authorities have set for him. The night ahead is a test, and he tells them the one thing that would carry them through it. They sleep instead. He is not doubting that they mean what they said at verse 31; he is saying that meaning it will not be enough.\f*
\v 39 And he went away again and prayed, saying the same word.
\v 40 And he came again and found them sleeping, for their eyes were weighed down, and they did not know what to answer him.
\v 41 And he came the third time and said to them, "Sleep on now and rest. It is enough. The hour has come. Look — the son of man is being handed over into the hands of sinners \add [i.e. three times he prays and three times they sleep, as three times Peter will deny him before morning]\add*.
\v 42 Get up, let us go. Look — the one handing me over has come near."
\s1 The Arrest
\p
\v 43 And immediately, while he was still speaking, Judas, one of the twelve, arrived, and with him a crowd with swords and clubs, from the chief priests and the scribes and the elders.
\v 44 Now the one handing him over had given them a signal, saying, "The one I kiss is the man. Seize him and lead him away under guard."
\v 45 And he came and immediately went up to him and said, "Rabbi," and kissed him warmly \add [i.e. the greeting of a student to his teacher, used as the mark for the arresting party]\add*.
\v 46 And they laid their hands on him and seized him.
\v 47 But one of those standing by drew his sword and struck the slave of the high priest and took off his ear.
\v 48 And Jesus answered them, "Have you come out with swords and clubs to arrest me, as against a bandit?\f + \fr 14:48 \ft The word is the one in "a cave of bandits" (11:17) — an armed insurrectionist, not a pickpocket. They have turned out a force fit for taking a guerrilla, at night, guided by an informer, against a man who has been teaching in the open every day.\f*
\v 49 Day after day I was with you in the temple, teaching, and you did not seize me. But — so that the scriptures may be filled full."
\v 50 And they all left him and fled \add [i.e. exactly as he said: strike the shepherd and the sheep scatter]\add*.
\v 51 And a certain young man was following along with him, wearing a linen cloth over his bare body; and they seized him,
\v 52 but he left the linen cloth behind and fled naked.\f + \fr 14:52 \ft The writer does not say who he is. What he does say is that this man was still following when everyone else had gone, and that even he gave up everything he had on to get away. "Even the bravest of warriors shall flee naked on that day" (Amos 2:16). The word for his linen cloth is the same word used for the cloth Jesus is buried in (15:46), and a young man will be sitting in the tomb wearing a white robe at the end of the book (16:5).\f*
\s1 Before the High Priest
\p
\v 53 And they led Jesus away to the high priest, and all the chief priests and the elders and the scribes came together.
\v 54 And Peter followed him from far off, right inside, into the courtyard of the high priest, and he was sitting with the attendants and warming himself at the fire \add [i.e. he has not run, and he is sitting among the household of the men trying him]\add*.
\v 55 And the chief priests and the whole council were seeking testimony against Jesus in order to put him to death, and they were not finding any.
\v 56 For many were giving false testimony against him, and their testimonies did not agree.\f + \fr 14:56 \ft The court is not weighing evidence; it has a verdict and needs testimony to carry it. And it cannot get any. A capital charge required at least two witnesses whose accounts matched (Deuteronomy 17:6; 19:15), and by that standard the proceeding has already failed twice over.\f*
\v 57 And some stood up and gave false testimony against him, saying,
\v 58 "We heard him say, 'I will pull down this sanctuary made with hands, and in three days I will build another, not made with hands.'"\f + \fr 14:58 \ft What he actually said was that not one stone would be left on another (13:2) — a prophecy about what would happen to it, turned here into a boast about what he would do to it. Even so, listen to the words they put in his mouth. "Made with hands" is the standing phrase in the Greek scriptures for an idol, a thing men construct and then trust (Leviticus 26:1; Isaiah 2:18; 46:6). The false witnesses cannot agree, and they have still said something true.\f*
\v 59 And not even so did their testimony agree.
\v 60 And the high priest stood up in the middle and questioned Jesus, saying, "Do you answer nothing? What is this that these men testify against you?"
\v 61 But he kept quiet and answered nothing.\f + \fr 14:61 \ft "He was oppressed and afflicted, yet he did not open his mouth; like a lamb led to the slaughter, and like a sheep silent before its shearers, so he did not open his mouth" (Isaiah 53:7). It is the day the lambs are killed.\f* Again the high priest questioned him and said to him, "Are you the Christ, the Son of the Blessed?" \add [i.e. "the Blessed" is a way of saying God without saying the name]\add*
\v 62 And Jesus said, "I am; and you will see the son of man sitting at the right hand of the Power and coming with the clouds of the sky."\f + \fr 14:62 \ft From the beginning of this book he has silenced everyone who named him — the demons, the healed, and the disciples after Peter's confession. He breaks that silence here, in the one place where saying it will kill him. And he does not stop at "I am": he puts together Psalm 110:1, the enthronement at God's right hand that he used against the scribes two days ago (12:36), with Daniel 7:13, the Son of Man brought to the Ancient of Days and given the kingdom. To the men sentencing him he says: you will see which of us God seats.\f*
\v 63 And the high priest tore his tunics \add [i.e. the prescribed response of a judge who has heard blasphemy]\add* and said, "What further need do we have of witnesses?
\v 64 You have heard the blasphemy. How does it look to you?" And they all condemned him as deserving death.
\v 65 And some began to spit on him and to cover his face and to punch him and to say to him, "Prophesy!" And the attendants took him with slaps. \add [i.e. they blindfold him and demand a prophecy, while down in the courtyard the prophecy he made about Peter is coming true word for word]\add*
\s1 Peter Denies Him
\p
\v 66 And while Peter was below in the courtyard, one of the servant girls of the high priest came,
\v 67 and when she saw Peter warming himself she looked straight at him and said, "You too were with the Nazarene, Jesus."
\v 68 But he denied it, saying, "I neither know nor understand what you are saying." And he went out into the forecourt; and a cock crowed.
\v 69 And the servant girl saw him and began again to say to those standing by, "This man is one of them."
\v 70 But he denied it again. And after a little while those standing by said to Peter again, "Truly you are one of them, for you are a Galilean too."
\v 71 But he began to call down curses and to swear, "I do not know this man you are talking about." \add [i.e. he swears an oath with a curse on himself if he is lying, and he will not even use the name]\add*
\v 72 And immediately a cock crowed a second time. And Peter remembered the word, how Jesus had said to him, "Before the cock crows twice, you will deny me three times." And he broke down and wept.\f + \fr 14:72 \ft The two scenes have been running side by side: upstairs, Jesus says who he is and is condemned for it; downstairs, Peter says he does not know him and goes free. The man who drew a sword on the high priest's slave a few hours earlier is undone by the high priest's serving girl.\f*

\c 15
\s1 Before Pilate
\p
\v 1 And immediately, early in the morning, the chief priests took counsel with the elders and the scribes and the whole council; and they bound Jesus and led him away and handed him over to Pilate \add [i.e. the Roman governor, the only man in the province who could order an execution]\add*.
\v 2 And Pilate questioned him, "Are you the king of the Jews?"\f + \fr 15:2 \ft The charge has been translated. Before the council the question was whether he was the Christ, the Son of the Blessed — a question about God. Before a Roman governor it becomes a claim to a throne, which is treason, and treason is a matter Rome kills for. His answer, "you say so," neither denies the title nor accepts the meaning being put on it.\f* And he answered him, "You say so."
\v 3 And the chief priests were accusing him of many things.
\v 4 And Pilate questioned him again, saying, "Do you answer nothing? Look how many things they accuse you of."
\v 5 But Jesus answered nothing more, so that Pilate marveled \add [i.e. under Roman procedure a man who mounted no defense could be convicted on that alone]\add*.
\s1 Barabbas
\p
\v 6 Now at the feast he used to release for them one prisoner, whomever they asked for.
\v 7 And there was one called Barabbas, bound with the rebels who had committed murder in the uprising.\f + \fr 15:7 \ft The name means, in Aramaic, "son of the father." The crowd is being offered a choice between two men and two ideas of what a king is for: one imprisoned for killing in an armed revolt against Rome, and one who rode in on a donkey and said the Son of Man came to give his life as a ransom.\f*
\v 8 And the crowd came up and began to ask him to do as he usually did for them.
\v 9 And Pilate answered them, "Do you want me to release for you the king of the Jews?"
\v 10 For he knew that the chief priests had handed him over out of envy \add [i.e. the writer's own verdict on their motive, whatever charge they filed]\add*.
\v 11 But the chief priests stirred up the crowd to have him release Barabbas for them instead \add [i.e. the same crowd that spread its cloaks on the road five days ago]\add*.
\v 12 And Pilate answered them again, "What then shall I do with the one you call the king of the Jews?"
\v 13 And they screamed out again, "Crucify him!"
\v 14 And Pilate said to them, "Why? What evil has he done?" But they screamed out all the more, "Crucify him!" \add [i.e. no crime is named, then or afterwards]\add*
\v 15 And Pilate, wanting to satisfy the crowd \add [i.e. the decision is crowd management, not law]\add*, released Barabbas for them; and he had Jesus flogged\f + \fr 15:15 \ft The standard preliminary to crucifixion: a whip of leather thongs weighted with bone or metal, which stripped the flesh off the back and could kill a man on its own. The writer gives it one word and moves on.\f* and handed him over to be crucified.
\s1 The Soldiers Crown Him
\p
\v 16 And the soldiers led him away inside the courtyard, that is, the praetorium \add [i.e. the governor's residence]\add*, and they called together the whole cohort \add [i.e. some six hundred men, so this is a performance with an audience]\add*.
\v 17 And they put purple on him, and they wove a crown of thorns and put it around his head.\f + \fr 15:17 \ft Every item is a piece of imperial ceremony played for laughs: the purple of a king's robe, a wreath for the head, a reed for a scepter, the knee bent in homage. They are staging a coronation in order to mock it. A reader who has come this far knows they are staging a real one.\f*
\v 18 And they began to salute him, "Hail, king of the Jews!" \add [i.e. their parody of the greeting given to Caesar]\add*
\v 19 And they were beating him on the head with a reed and spitting on him, and they knelt down and bowed to him.
\v 20 And when they had mocked him, they stripped the purple off him and put his own clothes on him. And they led him out to crucify him. \add [i.e. mocked, spat on, flogged, and killed, in the order he gave the twelve on the road]\add*
\s1 The Crucifixion
\p
\v 21 And they pressed into service a passer-by, Simon of Cyrene, coming in from the countryside, the father of Alexander and Rufus, to carry his cross.\f + \fr 15:21 \ft Roman soldiers could conscript a civilian on the spot. What he carries is the crossbeam; the upright already stands at the place. Two things are worth noticing. He is named by his sons, which is only useful if the readers know Alexander and Rufus — this is someone's father, in someone's congregation. And he is a stranger walking in from the fields, doing under compulsion the one thing the twelve were told to do and are not there to do (8:34).\f*
\v 22 And they brought him to the place Golgotha, which translated is Place of a Skull \add [i.e. outside the city wall, where Rome carried out its executions]\add*.
\v 23 And they were offering him wine mixed with myrrh, but he did not take it \add [i.e. it would have dulled the pain, and he had said he would not drink again]\add*.
\v 24 And they crucified him, and they divided his garments among them, throwing lots over them for who should take what.\f + \fr 15:24 \ft "And they crucified him" — three words in Greek, and no description of any of it. His readers had seen crosses; nothing needed saying. What he does spell out is the soldiers dividing the clothes, because that is Psalm 22:18: "they divide my garments among them, and for my clothing they cast lots." From here to the death, this psalm supplies the language of the scene, and it is never quoted openly until the last cry.\f*
\v 25 And it was the third hour when they crucified him \add [i.e. about nine in the morning]\add*.
\v 26 And the inscription of the charge against him was written up: The King of the Jews.\f + \fr 15:26 \ft Roman practice was to post the offence for which a man was being executed, carried before him on the way and fixed above him at the place. The state has published the truth about him on the instrument of his death, and everyone who walks past can read it.\f*
\v 27 And with him they crucified two bandits, one at his right and one at his left.\f + \fr 15:27 \ft "One at your right and one at your left, in your glory" is exactly what James and John asked for (10:37). He told them they did not know what they were asking. This is what those places turn out to be. And it is what Isaiah said of the servant: "he was numbered with the transgressors" (Isaiah 53:12).\f*
\s1 Mocked on the Cross
\p
\v 29 And those passing by were blaspheming him, wagging their heads\f + \fr 15:29 \ft "All who see me mock me; they make mouths at me, they wag their heads" (Psalm 22:7).\f* and saying, "Aha! You who pull down the sanctuary and build it in three days,
\v 30 rescue yourself and come down from the cross!"
\v 31 In the same way the chief priests also, mocking among themselves with the scribes, were saying, "He rescued others; himself he cannot rescue.\f + \fr 15:31 \ft They mean it as proof that he was a fraud. Taken exactly as they say it, it is the truest sentence anyone speaks in this book. He cannot do both. Rescuing others is what makes rescuing himself impossible, because a ransom that is not paid ransoms no one (10:45).\f*
\v 32 Let the Christ, the king of Israel, come down now from the cross, so that we may see and trust." \add [i.e. show us first and we will believe — which is the reverse of everything he has said trust is]\add* And those crucified with him were reviling him.
\s1 Darkness, the Cry, and the Torn Curtain
\p
\v 33 And when the sixth hour came, darkness came over the whole land until the ninth hour.\f + \fr 15:33 \ft Noon to three. "I will make the sun go down at noon and darken the earth in broad daylight; I will turn your feasts into mourning... I will make it like the mourning for an only son, and the end of it like a bitter day" (Amos 8:9-10). A feast turned to mourning, an only son, darkness at midday — and behind that, the darkness over Egypt in the plague before the first Passover (Exodus 10:21-23).\f*
\v 34 And at the ninth hour Jesus shouted with a great voice, "Eloi, Eloi, lema sabachthani?" which translated is, "My God, my God, why have you abandoned me?"\f + \fr 15:34 \ft The first line of Psalm 22, spoken in Aramaic. Quoting a psalm's opening line was a way of invoking the whole of it, and the whole of it has been running underneath this scene already — the wagging heads, the divided clothes, the taunt to let God rescue him. But Psalm 22 does not stay where it starts. It turns at verse 21, "you have rescued me," and ends with the news carried to a people not yet born and "all the ends of the earth shall remember and turn to the LORD, and all the families of the nations shall worship before him" (22:27, 30-31). The abandonment is real; he is not reciting. And it is the first line of a psalm that ends with the nations coming in — and the next voice in this chapter is a Roman's.\f*
\v 35 And some of those standing by heard it and said, "Look, he is calling Elijah." \add [i.e. Eloi sounded to them like Elijah, whom popular belief expected to come to the aid of the righteous]\add*
\v 36 And someone ran and filled a sponge with sour wine, put it on a reed, and gave him a drink, saying, "Leave him. Let us see whether Elijah comes to take him down."\f + \fr 15:36 \ft "For my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink" (Psalm 69:21), another psalm of a righteous man destroyed by the people around him. Elijah does not come. In this book Elijah has already come, and they killed him too (9:13).\f*
\v 37 But Jesus let out a great cry and breathed out his last \add [i.e. a shout, not the whisper of a man exhausted]\add*.
\v 38 And the curtain of the sanctuary was torn in two, from top to bottom.\f + \fr 15:38 \ft The curtain closing off the Holy of Holies, which one man entered one day a year, and no one else ever (Leviticus 16:2). "Torn" is \tl schizō\tl*, and it has been used once before in this book — the heavens torn open at the river, with the voice declaring him God's Son (1:10). The book is bracketed by two tearings. And "from top to bottom" says who did it: not from where men could reach. Everything since chapter 11 has been the judgment of this building, and here it is opened, from God's side, and left open.\f*
\v 39 And when the centurion standing opposite him saw that he breathed out his last like this, he said, "Truly this man was God's Son."\f + \fr 15:39 \ft The last and largest of the ironies. God said it at the river and on the mountain, the demons said it and were silenced, Peter got halfway to it and recoiled from what it meant — and the first human being in this book to say it plainly is the Roman officer in charge of the execution, a Gentile, standing at the foot of a cross. He does not say it because of a miracle. He says it because of how the man died. The temple was to be a house of prayer for all the nations and was not; its curtain has just been torn, and one of the nations is standing outside the city saying this.\f*
\s1 The Women Watching
\p
\v 40 And there were also women watching from far off, among them Mary the Magdalene, and Mary the mother of James the little and of Joses, and Salome,
\v 41 who used to follow him and serve him when he was in Galilee, and many other women who had come up with him to Jerusalem.\f + \fr 15:41 \ft Two verbs, and both are loaded. They followed him — the word for discipleship in this book from the first call by the lake (1:18). And they served him — the word he used for what the Son of Man came to do (10:45). While the twelve were arguing about rank, these women were doing the thing he defined greatness as, and they are the ones still here. They will see the burial and they will come to the tomb; they are the thread that runs from the cross to the empty grave.\f*
\s1 The Burial
\p
\v 42 And when evening had already come — since it was Preparation, that is, the day before the Sabbath —\f + \fr 15:42 \ft The body had to be down and buried before nightfall, because the law forbids leaving an executed man hanging overnight (Deuteronomy 21:22-23), and because the Sabbath was about to begin and nothing could be done during it. That is why the burial is hurried and why the women come back on Sunday morning with spices.\f*
\v 43 Joseph of Arimathea came, a man of standing on the council, who was himself also waiting for the kingdom of God; and he took courage and went in to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus.\f + \fr 15:43 \ft He sits on the council that condemned him. Asking a Roman governor for the corpse of a man executed for claiming a throne is not a formality; it puts your own name beside his. The writer says he took courage, and it is worth setting that beside the twelve, who are nowhere in this chapter.\f*
\v 44 And Pilate marveled that he had already died, and he called the centurion to him and asked him whether he had been dead long \add [i.e. crucifixion normally took days; six hours was fast]\add*.
\v 45 And when he learned it from the centurion, he granted the corpse to Joseph.
\v 46 And he bought a linen cloth and took him down and wrapped him in the linen and laid him in a tomb that had been cut out of rock, and he rolled a stone against the door of the tomb.\f + \fr 15:46 \ft A tomb cut into rock with a stone to seal it belonged to a family with money — "they made his grave with the wicked, and with a rich man in his death" (Isaiah 53:9). He has been executed between two insurrectionists and buried in a wealthy councillor's tomb, and the servant song has both halves.\f*
\v 47 And Mary the Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses were watching where he was laid \add [i.e. so that there could be no question afterwards which tomb it was]\add*.

\c 16
\s1 He Has Been Raised
\p
\v 1 And when the Sabbath had passed, Mary the Magdalene and Mary the mother of James and Salome bought spices, so that they might go and rub him with them \add [i.e. to finish a burial that Friday evening had cut short]\add*.
\v 2 And very early on the first day of the week they came to the tomb, when the sun had risen.
\v 3 And they were saying to one another, "Who will roll away the stone for us from the door of the tomb?" \add [i.e. they are not expecting anything; they are expecting a corpse and a problem]\add*
\v 4 And they looked up and saw that the stone had been rolled away — for it was very large.
\v 5 And they went into the tomb and saw a young man sitting on the right side, wearing a white robe, and they were astonished.\f + \fr 16:5 \ft He is not called an angel; he is called a young man, the same word used of the follower who was seized at the arrest, left his linen cloth behind, and ran away naked (14:51-52). Whether the writer means the same person or only means us to hear the echo, the shape is the same: a man stripped in the dark now sits clothed in white in the place of death. White clothing in Israel's visions belongs to heaven (Daniel 7:9), and it is what Jesus' own garments became on the mountain (9:3).\f*
\v 6 And he said to them, "Do not be astonished. You are looking for Jesus the Nazarene, the crucified one \add [i.e. the crucifixion is not cancelled by this; it is part of who he now is]\add*. He has been raised. He is not here. Look — the place where they laid him.
\v 7 But go, tell his disciples and Peter\f + \fr 16:7 \ft "And Peter." He is not lumped in with the rest and he is not left out; he is named on his own, three days after swearing with curses that he had never met the man. The last thing Jesus said before the arrest was that after he was raised he would go ahead of them into Galilee (14:28), and the promise is repeated here to the man who broke worst.\f* that he is going ahead of you into Galilee. There you will see him, just as he told you."\f + \fr 16:7 \ft Galilee is where it began — the lake, the four fishermen, the first announcement that God's reign had come near. The shepherd was struck and the sheep scattered; now the shepherd goes ahead of them again, back to the beginning, to start them over. "Just as he told you" is the writer's closing verdict: every single thing this man said would happen has happened.\f*
\v 8 And they went out and fled from the tomb, for trembling and amazement had hold of them; and they said nothing to anyone, for they were afraid.\f + \fr 16:8 \ft The oldest and best manuscripts end the book here, in the middle of the women's fear, and this is almost certainly where the writer stopped. Everything printed from verse 9 on is absent from those copies; Eusebius in the fourth century said it was missing from nearly all the manuscripts he knew of, and Jerome said the same. Its vocabulary and manner are not this writer's, and its contents read as a summary gathered from the resurrection accounts in the other gospels. It is old enough to be worth having, and it is printed here, but it is an appendix. And consider what the writer's own ending does. All through this book people ordered to keep silent could not stop talking. Here, for the first time, someone is ordered to speak — and does not. The message the women failed to carry is the message the reader has just been handed.\f*
\s1 He Appears to Those Who Had Been with Him
\p
\v 9 Now when he had risen early on the first day of the week, he appeared first to Mary the Magdalene, from whom he had thrown out seven demons.
\v 10 She went and reported it to those who had been with him, as they were mourning and weeping.
\v 11 And when they heard that he was alive and had been seen by her, they did not trust it.
\v 12 And after these things he was made plain in another form to two of them as they were walking, on their way into the country.
\v 13 And they went off and reported it to the rest; and they did not trust them either.
\s1 Go into the Whole World
\p
\v 14 Later he was made plain to the eleven themselves as they were reclining at table, and he reproached their lack of trust and their hardness of heart, because they had not trusted those who saw him after he had been raised.
\v 15 And he said to them, "Go into the whole world and proclaim the good news to the whole creation.
\v 16 Whoever trusts and is immersed will be rescued; but whoever does not trust will be condemned.
\v 17 And these signs will follow along with those who trust: in my name they will throw out demons; they will speak with new tongues;
\v 18 they will pick up serpents in their hands; and if they drink anything deadly it will not harm them; they will lay hands on the sick, and they will be well."\f + \fr 16:18 \ft The list has no parallel anywhere else in this book and reads as a summary of what the early communities had in fact seen — new tongues at Pentecost, Paul shaking a viper off his hand on Malta (Acts 2:4; 28:3-6). It is a description of what happened, not an instruction to go looking for snakes or poison.\f*
\s1 He Is Taken Up
\p
\v 19 So then the Lord Jesus, after he had spoken to them, was taken up into heaven and sat down at the right hand of God.\f + \fr 16:19 \ft Psalm 110:1 again — the seat at God's right hand that he claimed before the high priest and that cost him his life (12:36; 14:62).\f*
\v 20 And they went out and proclaimed everywhere, the Lord working with them and confirming the word through the signs that followed along.
